window.DATA = {"exam":{"date":"2026-11-20","questions":100,"minutes":240,"pass":70},"domains":[{"id":"01-sitework","n":1,"name":"Sitework","items":9,"books":"study-only","blurb":"Soil types, sloping and benching, trench protection, pipe bedding and grading.","ref":"<p>Main source: <em>Pipe and Excavation Contracting</em> (1987 or 2011, <strong>study only</strong>). In-room backup: OSHA Subpart P (excavations), IRC R401 (grading and drainage), IBC Ch. 18 and Appendix J (grading), IRC Ch. 30 / plumbing sewer slopes.</p>","html":"<h2>Key concepts</h2>\n<p><strong>Soil and earthwork</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bank (in-place) volume is what's in the ground. Loose volume is after digging (it swells). Compacted volume is after compaction (it shrinks).</li>\n<li>Loose volume = bank volume × (1 + swell %).</li>\n<li>Typical swell: sand and gravel 10–15%; common earth 20–30%; clay 30–40%; rock 50% or more.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Compaction</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lab test: Proctor (standard ASTM D698, modified ASTM D1557), which sets maximum dry density at optimum moisture.</li>\n<li>Field tests: nuclear density gauge, sand cone.</li>\n<li>Specs are usually 90–95% relative compaction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Compaction equipment</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sheepsfoot or padfoot roller: cohesive soils (clay).</li>\n<li>Vibratory smooth-drum roller or plate compactor: granular soils (sand, gravel).</li>\n<li>Pneumatic (rubber-tired) roller: mixed soils, proof-rolling.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Earthwork volume: average end area method</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>V (cu yd) = [(A1 + A2) ÷ 2 × L] ÷ 27, with areas in sq ft and length in ft.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Surveying</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Height of instrument (HI) = benchmark elevation + backsight.</li>\n<li>Elevation = HI − foresight.</li>\n<li>Grade stakes are marked C (cut) or F (fill) with the depth.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Pipe</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bedding supports the bottom of the pipe. Haunching fills the zone from the bedding up to the springline (mid-pipe), and it's the most important zone for flexible pipe. Initial backfill runs to about 12 in over the crown.</li>\n<li>Rigid pipe: RCP, VCP, DIP (semi). Flexible pipe: PVC, HDPE.</li>\n<li>Thrust blocks go at bends, tees, caps and valves on pressure pipe.</li>\n<li>Building sewer slope (IRC P3005.3 / IPC 704.1): 2½ in or less at ¼ in/ft; 3–6 in at ⅛ in/ft.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Dewatering.</strong> Sumps and pumps; wellpoints for granular soils; deep wells.</p>\n<p><strong>Arizona specifics</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Arizona 811 (Blue Stake):</strong> call at least <strong>2 full working days</strong> before you excavate (A.R.S. § 40-360.22).</li>\n<li>Stormwater: an AZPDES Construction General Permit and SWPPP from ADEQ are required when disturbing <strong>1 acre or more</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Grading and drainage (IRC R401.3)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fall of at least 6 in within the first 10 ft from the foundation (5%).</li>\n<li>Impervious surfaces within 10 ft: at least 2% slope away.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>IBC Appendix J (grading, where adopted)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cut and fill slopes no steeper than 2H:1V.</li>\n<li>Fill compacted to 90% of maximum dry density.</li>\n</ul>","qcount":13},{"id":"02-concrete","n":2,"name":"Concrete","items":14,"books":"study-only","blurb":"Cement types, water-cement ratio, testing, curing, joints and rebar. The biggest domain.","ref":"<p>Main source: <em>Design and Control of Concrete Mixtures</em> (PCA, 2011 or 2016 edition, <strong>study only</strong>). In-room backup: IBC Ch. 19 (points to ACI 318), IRC R402.2, R403, R506, and OSHA Subpart Q.</p>","html":"<h2>Key concepts</h2>\n<p><strong>Portland cement types (ASTM C150)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I</td>\n<td>Normal, general purpose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>II</td>\n<td>Moderate sulfate resistance, moderate heat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>III</td>\n<td>High early strength</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IV</td>\n<td>Low heat of hydration (mass concrete)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>V</td>\n<td>High sulfate resistance</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Arizona soils are often high in sulfates, so Type II/V cement is common here.</p>\n<p><strong>Water-cement ratio (w/c).</strong> This is the single biggest factor in strength and durability. Lower w/c means higher strength, lower permeability and better durability (Abrams' law). Adding water on site to raise slump lowers strength.</p>\n<p><strong>Supplementary cementitious materials (SCMs):</strong> fly ash (Class F, Class C), slag cement, silica fume. They improve long-term strength and sulfate resistance and lower heat.</p>\n<p><strong>Admixtures</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Admixture</th>\n<th>Does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Air-entraining</td>\n<td>Microscopic bubbles for freeze-thaw resistance (typically 4–8% air)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Water reducer / plasticizer</td>\n<td>Same slump with less water</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High-range water reducer (superplasticizer)</td>\n<td>Flowing concrete, very low w/c</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retarder</td>\n<td>Slows set (hot weather, long hauls)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Accelerator</td>\n<td>Speeds set (cold weather). Calcium chloride is the classic one, but it promotes corrosion of reinforcing steel and is not used in prestressed concrete</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Aggregates.</strong> Maximum size is the smallest of: 1/5 of the narrowest form dimension, 1/3 of the slab depth, or 3/4 of the clear spacing between bars.</p>\n<p><strong>Tests</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Test</th>\n<th>Standard</th>\n<th>Key facts</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Slump</td>\n<td>ASTM C143</td>\n<td>12 in cone; 3 layers, 25 rod strokes each; measures consistency/workability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cylinders</td>\n<td>ASTM C31 (make), C39 (break)</td>\n<td>6×12 in or 4×8 in; standard strength age is 28 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Air content</td>\n<td>ASTM C231 (pressure), C173 (volumetric)</td>\n<td>Volumetric method for lightweight aggregate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unit weight</td>\n<td>ASTM C138</td>\n<td>Normal-weight concrete is about 145–150 lb/cu ft</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sampling fresh concrete</td>\n<td>ASTM C172</td>\n<td>Take samples from the middle of the batch</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Strength gain.</strong> Roughly 65–70% of 28-day strength at 7 days, with proper curing.</p>\n<p><strong>Curing.</strong> Keep the concrete moist and warm. Methods: ponding, wet burlap, curing compound, plastic sheeting. Seven days is the common minimum for Type I.</p>\n<p><strong>Hot weather (ACI 305).</strong> Risk of rapid evaporation and plastic shrinkage cracks. Use cool water or ice, retarders, windbreaks, fog spray and evaporation control.</p>\n<p><strong>Cold weather (ACI 306).</strong> Applies when air temperature is or will fall below 40°F. Protect concrete from freezing until it reaches about 500 psi. Use heated materials, insulated blankets and accelerators.</p>\n<p><strong>Placing and finishing.</strong> Don't drop concrete from excessive height, which causes segregation. Consolidate with vibrators. Sequence: screed, then bull float, wait for bleed water to evaporate, then edge, joint, float and trowel. Never finish while bleed water is on the surface; it causes dusting and scaling.</p>\n<p><strong>Joints</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Control (contraction) joints: depth about 1/4 of slab thickness; spacing about 24–36 × slab thickness (for example, a 4 in slab gets joints every 8–12 ft).</li>\n<li>Isolation (expansion) joints: around columns and at walls.</li>\n<li>Construction joints: where a pour stops.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reinforcing</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bar number is the diameter in eighths of an inch (#4 = ½ in, #5 = ⅝ in).</li>\n<li>Grade 60 = 60,000 psi yield.</li>\n<li>Minimum cover (ACI 318): 3 in when cast against earth; 2 in for #6 and larger exposed to earth or weather (1½ in for #5 and smaller); ¾ in for slabs and walls not exposed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Volume math.</strong> Cubic yards = (L × W × D in feet) ÷ 27.</p>\n<h2>Code locations</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>Where</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimum f'c by weathering</td>\n<td>IRC Table R402.2 (2,500 / 3,000 / 3,500 psi)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Concrete per ACI 318</td>\n<td>IBC 1901.2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Footing size and depth</td>\n<td>IRC R403.1, Table R403.1; IBC 1809</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slab on ground (3½ in min, 6-mil vapor retarder, 4 in base)</td>\n<td>IRC R506</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Special inspection of concrete</td>\n<td>IBC 1705.3, Table 1705.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Formwork, rebar caps, shoring</td>\n<td>OSHA 1926.701–.703</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>","qcount":18},{"id":"03-masonry","n":3,"name":"Masonry","items":10,"books":"study-only","blurb":"Mortar types, CMU, grout, reinforcement and weather limits.","ref":"<p>Main source: <em>Modern Masonry</em> (8th or 9th edition, <strong>study only</strong>). In-room backup: IBC Ch. 21 (points to TMS 402/602), IRC R606–R608 and R703.8 (veneer), OSHA 1926.706.</p>","html":"<h2>Key concepts</h2>\n<p><strong>Mortar types (ASTM C270).</strong> Memory trick: <strong>M a S o N w O r K</strong>. Strongest to weakest:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Approx. min. compressive strength</th>\n<th>Typical use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>2,500 psi</td>\n<td>Below grade, foundations, heavy loads, retaining walls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>S</td>\n<td>1,800 psi</td>\n<td>Structural, below grade, high lateral loads (wind, seismic)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>750 psi</td>\n<td>General-purpose exterior above grade, veneer, parapets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>O</td>\n<td>350 psi</td>\n<td>Interior non-load-bearing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>K</td>\n<td>75 psi</td>\n<td>Historic restoration</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Mortar handling.</strong> Retemper (add water) only to replace evaporation. Discard mortar not used within <strong>2½ hours</strong> of mixing.</p>\n<p><strong>Grout (ASTM C476).</strong> Fine grout or coarse grout, placed at a slump of 8–11 in. Grout pours over about 5 ft high need cleanouts at the bottom of the cells (high-lift grouting).</p>\n<p><strong>Units</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Unit</th>\n<th>Nominal</th>\n<th>Actual</th>\n<th>Per sq ft of wall</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>CMU</td>\n<td>8 × 8 × 16 in</td>\n<td>7⅝ × 7⅝ × 15⅝ in</td>\n<td>1.125 (112.5 per 100 sq ft)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Modular brick</td>\n<td>4 × 2⅔ × 8 in</td>\n<td>3⅝ × 2¼ × 7⅝ in</td>\n<td>about 6.75 (675 per 100 sq ft)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Standard mortar joint: ⅜ in. Nominal size = actual size + joint.</p>\n<p><strong>Brick positions</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Position</th>\n<th>How it's laid</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Stretcher</td>\n<td>Horizontal, long face exposed (the most common)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Header</td>\n<td>Horizontal, end exposed (ties wythes together)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rowlock</td>\n<td>On edge, end exposed (sills, caps)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Soldier</td>\n<td>Standing on end, narrow face exposed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sailor</td>\n<td>Standing on end, wide (bed) face exposed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shiner</td>\n<td>On edge, wide (bed) face exposed horizontally</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Bonds</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Running bond: half-lap.</li>\n<li>Common (American) bond: a header course every 5th, 6th or 7th course.</li>\n<li>Flemish bond: alternating headers and stretchers in each course.</li>\n<li>English bond: alternating courses of headers and stretchers.</li>\n<li>Stack bond: no overlap, so it needs horizontal joint reinforcement.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Joints.</strong> Concave and V-tooled joints are the most weather-resistant because tooling compacts the mortar. Raked and struck joints hold water; avoid them on exterior walls exposed to weather.</p>\n<p><strong>Movement</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Clay brick expands over time (moisture), so it gets <strong>expansion joints</strong>.</li>\n<li>Concrete block shrinks, so it gets <strong>control joints</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Problems</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Efflorescence: white deposit of soluble salts carried to the surface by water. Fix the water source; dry-brush, then wash.</li>\n<li>Freeze-thaw spalling.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Weather (TMS 602)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cold-weather procedures apply below 40°F: heat materials and protect the work.</li>\n<li>Hot-weather procedures apply above 100°F, or above 90°F with wind over 8 mph: wet the units and limit how far mortar is spread ahead.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Veneer (IRC R703.8)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>1 in minimum air space.</li>\n<li>Ties: each supports max 2.67 sq ft, spaced max 32 in horizontally and 24 in vertically.</li>\n<li>Weep holes: max 33 in o.c., at least 3/16 in diameter, above the flashing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Testing.</strong> f'm is the specified compressive strength of masonry, verified by prism tests or the unit-strength method.</p>\n<h2>Code locations</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>Where</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Masonry design and specs (TMS 402/602)</td>\n<td>IBC 2101.2, 2105</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mortar</td>\n<td>IBC 2103.2; IRC R606.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Masonry veneer</td>\n<td>IRC R703.8; IBC 1404.6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Masonry fireplaces and chimneys</td>\n<td>IRC Ch. 10; IBC 2111–2113</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Special inspection</td>\n<td>IBC 1705.4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Limited access zone; bracing walls over 8 ft</td>\n<td>OSHA 1926.706</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>","qcount":16},{"id":"04-metals","n":4,"name":"Metals","items":13,"books":"study-only","blurb":"Steel joists, bridging, erection rules and OSHA Subpart R.","ref":"<p>Main source: <em>Handling and Erection of Steel Joists and Joist Girders</em> (SJI, 2006 or 2008, <strong>study only</strong>). In-room backup: OSHA 1926 Subpart R (1926.750–.761) and IBC Ch. 22.</p>","html":"<h2>Key concepts</h2>\n<p><strong>Joist series (SJI)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Series</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>K</td>\n<td>Open-web steel joists, short span (up to about 60 ft)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LH</td>\n<td>Longspan joists</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DLH</td>\n<td>Deep longspan joists</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Joist girders</td>\n<td>Primary members supporting joists at panel points</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CJ</td>\n<td>Composite joists</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Minimum bearing (SJI)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Series</th>\n<th>On steel</th>\n<th>On masonry or concrete</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>K</td>\n<td>2½ in</td>\n<td>4 in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LH / DLH</td>\n<td>4 in</td>\n<td>6 in</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>End anchorage</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>K-series: two ⅛ in fillet welds 1 in long (one each side of the seat), or a ½ in bolt.</li>\n<li>LH/DLH: two ¼ in fillet welds 2 in long, or ¾ in bolts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Bridging.</strong> Horizontal (continuous angles welded to chords) or diagonal (cross-bracing, bolted or welded). Bridging holds the chords in line and prevents lateral buckling. Bridging ends must be anchored to a wall or beam.</p>\n<p><strong>Erection bridging.</strong> SJI tables flag long or heavy joists (the red-shaded spans) that need bolted diagonal bridging installed <strong>before the hoisting line is released</strong>. OSHA mirrors this in 1926.757 and its Tables A and B.</p>\n<p><strong>Handling</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lift and store joists upright, on blocking, off the ground.</li>\n<li>Never lift by the bridging or web members.</li>\n<li>Use spreader bars for long joists.</li>\n<li>Don't drop or drag them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Construction loads</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>No loads go on joists until bridging is installed and anchored and the joist ends are attached (1926.757(e)).</li>\n<li>Place loads within 1 ft of the secured end or over joist girders at panel points.</li>\n<li>Don't exceed the joist's rated capacity.</li>\n<li>Bundles of decking: spread them out, and land them only where the joist can carry them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Structural steel</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Standard</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>W-shapes</td>\n<td>ASTM A992, Fy = 50 ksi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Angles, plates (common)</td>\n<td>ASTM A36, Fy = 36 ksi</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High-strength bolts</td>\n<td>ASTM F3125 (Grades A325, A490)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Structural welding</td>\n<td>AWS D1.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Steel design and specs</td>\n<td>AISC 360 (referenced by IBC 2205)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>OSHA Subpart R numbers</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>4 anchor rods per column (755(a)(1)).</li>\n<li>2 bolts per connection before releasing the hoisting line, wrench-tight (756(a)(1)).</li>\n<li>Fall protection at 15 ft (760(a)(1)).</li>\n<li>Connectors: protected above 2 stories or 30 ft (760(b)).</li>\n<li>Controlled decking zone: 15–30 ft only, max 90 ft × 90 ft (760(c)).</li>\n<li>Concrete or mortar must reach 75% of design strength before steel erection on footings and piers (752(a)(1)).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Code locations</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>Where</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Steel (references AISC 360)</td>\n<td>IBC 2205</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Steel joists (references SJI 100)</td>\n<td>IBC 2207</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cold-formed steel framing</td>\n<td>IBC 2210–2211; IRC R505, R603, R804</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Special inspection of steel</td>\n<td>IBC 1705.2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Steel erection safety</td>\n<td>OSHA 1926.750–.761</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>","qcount":15},{"id":"05-carpentry","n":5,"name":"Carpentry","items":12,"books":"study-only","blurb":"Framing, spans, headers, sheathing and fasteners.","ref":"<p>Main source: <em>Carpentry and Building Construction</em> (2010 or 2016, <strong>study only</strong>). In-room backup: IRC Ch. 5 (floors), 6 (walls), 8 (roofs), R317 (decay); IBC Ch. 23 (wood).</p>","html":"<h2>Key concepts</h2>\n<p><strong>Nominal vs actual lumber</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Nominal</th>\n<th>Actual</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>2×4</td>\n<td>1½ × 3½</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2×6</td>\n<td>1½ × 5½</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2×8</td>\n<td>1½ × 7¼</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2×10</td>\n<td>1½ × 9¼</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2×12</td>\n<td>1½ × 11¼</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4×4</td>\n<td>3½ × 3½</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Board feet</strong> = (thickness in × width in × length ft) ÷ 12, using nominal dimensions.</p>\n<p><strong>Moisture.</strong> Grade stamp \"S-DRY\" means surfaced at 19% moisture content or less. Wood shrinks mostly across the grain, not along it.</p>\n<p><strong>Common nails (penny)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Size</th>\n<th>Length</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>6d</td>\n<td>2 in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8d</td>\n<td>2½ in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10d</td>\n<td>3 in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16d</td>\n<td>3½ in</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Panels.</strong> A span rating of 32/16 means max 32 in roof support spacing and 16 in floor spacing. Leave a ⅛ in gap at panel edges and ends. OSB and plywood are both rated by APA.</p>\n<p><strong>Engineered wood.</strong> LVL, PSL and glulam beams; I-joists (never cut or notch the flanges; holes go in the web only, per the manufacturer). Glulam is installed camber up.</p>\n<p><strong>Framing systems.</strong> Platform framing is standard: each floor is a platform for the next. Balloon framing has studs running continuously past the floors, so it needs fireblocking at each floor.</p>\n<p><strong>Roof framing</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pitch is rise per 12 in of run.</li>\n<li>Common rafter length = √(rise² + run²).</li>\n<li>Ceiling joists or rafter ties resist thrust.</li>\n<li>Collar ties go in the upper third of the attic space.</li>\n<li>A ridge beam is structural; a ridge board is not.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Stairs.</strong> Riser count = total rise ÷ target riser height, rounded up. Tread count = risers − 1.</p>\n<h2>IRC numbers to know</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>Rule</th>\n<th>Cite</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Anchor bolts</td>\n<td>½ in dia; max 6 ft o.c.; within 12 in of each plate end; 7 in embed; min 2 per plate section</td>\n<td>R403.1.6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Studs, bearing wall: notch</td>\n<td>Max 25% of stud width</td>\n<td>R602.6(1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Studs, bearing wall: bored hole</td>\n<td>Max 40% of width (60% if doubled, ≤2 successive); ≥ ⅝ in from edge</td>\n<td>R602.6(1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Studs, non-bearing: notch / bore</td>\n<td>40% / 60%</td>\n<td>R602.6(2)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Joists: notches</td>\n<td>Max 1/6 depth; not in middle 1/3 of span; ends max 1/4 depth</td>\n<td>R502.8.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Joists: bored holes</td>\n<td>Max 1/3 depth; ≥ 2 in from top/bottom edge</td>\n<td>R502.8.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Top plate</td>\n<td>Double; end joints offset ≥ 24 in</td>\n<td>R602.3.2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cut top plate (pipes)</td>\n<td>Steel strap 1½ in wide, 0.054 in thick, 6 in past each side</td>\n<td>R602.6.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fireblocking</td>\n<td>At ceiling and floor levels; at 10 ft intervals vertically and horizontally</td>\n<td>R302.11</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Draftstopping</td>\n<td>Floor-ceiling assemblies: max 1,000 sq ft areas</td>\n<td>R302.12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decay protection</td>\n<td>PT or naturally durable wood: joists within 18 in or beams within 12 in of exposed ground in crawl space; framing on foundation within 8 in of exposed earth; sills on concrete in contact with ground</td>\n<td>R317.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Joist bearing</td>\n<td>1½ in on wood or metal; 3 in on masonry or concrete</td>\n<td>R502.6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Collar ties</td>\n<td>Upper third of attic space</td>\n<td>R802.4.6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Headers</td>\n<td>Table R602.7(1)</td>\n<td>R602.7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stud size/spacing</td>\n<td>Table R602.3(5)</td>\n<td>R602.3.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Floor joist spans</td>\n<td>Tables R502.3.1(1)–(2)</td>\n<td>R502.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rafter spans</td>\n<td>Tables R802.4.1(1)–(8)</td>\n<td>R802.4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fastening schedule</td>\n<td>Table R602.3(1)</td>\n<td>R602.3</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>","qcount":18},{"id":"06-thermal-moisture","n":6,"name":"Thermal & Moisture Protection","items":10,"books":"in-room","blurb":"Roofing, flashing, insulation, vapor control and the building envelope.","ref":"<p>These questions are answerable from the books in the room: IRC Ch. 7 (wall covering), Ch. 8 (attic ventilation), Ch. 9 (roofing), Ch. 11 / N11 (energy), R406 (dampproofing); IBC Ch. 14 (exterior walls), Ch. 15 (roofing), Ch. 7 (fire-resistance and firestopping).</p>","html":"<h2>Key concepts</h2>\n<p><strong>Vapor retarder classes (IRC R702.7 / IBC 1404.3)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Class</th>\n<th>Permeance</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I</td>\n<td>0.1 perm or less</td>\n<td>Polyethylene sheet, foil</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>II</td>\n<td>Over 0.1 up to 1.0 perm</td>\n<td>Kraft-faced batts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>III</td>\n<td>Over 1.0 up to 10 perm</td>\n<td>Latex paint</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Phoenix and the Valley are in Climate Zone 2B</strong> (hot-dry). IRC Table N1102.1.2:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ceiling: R-38</li>\n<li>Wood-frame wall: R-13</li>\n<li>Floor: R-13</li>\n<li>Fenestration: U-0.40, SHGC 0.25</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Attic ventilation (IRC R806)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Net free vent area: 1/150 of the vented space.</li>\n<li>1/300 is allowed if 40–50% of the venting is in the upper portion (within 3 ft of the ridge).</li>\n<li>Vent openings: protected with screens of 1/16 to 1/4 in.</li>\n<li>Attic access: 22 × 30 in minimum (R807).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Roofing slope minimums</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Covering</th>\n<th>Min slope</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Asphalt shingles</td>\n<td>2:12</td>\n<td>Double underlayment from 2:12 to under 4:12 (IRC R905.2.2)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clay or concrete tile</td>\n<td>2½:12</td>\n<td>Double underlayment from 2½:12 to under 4:12 (IRC R905.3.2)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Built-up roof (BUR)</td>\n<td>¼:12</td>\n<td>Coal-tar BUR ⅛:12 (IBC 1507.10.1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wood shingles / shakes</td>\n<td>3:12</td>\n<td>IRC R905.7.1, R905.8.2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Roofing details</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Drip edge: lap 2 in, nail max 12 in o.c. (R905.2.8.5).</li>\n<li>Chimney cricket: needed when the chimney is over 30 in wide parallel to the ridge (R903.2.2).</li>\n<li>Re-covering: not allowed over two or more existing layers (R908.3.1.1).</li>\n<li>Asphalt shingles: 4 fasteners per strip shingle, more in high-wind areas (R905.2.6).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Walls</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A water-resistive barrier (#15 felt or approved) goes behind the siding (R703.2).</li>\n<li>Flashing laps shingle-fashion, upper over lower: at windows and doors, wall-roof intersections, and beneath the first course of masonry (R703.4).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Stucco</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Three coats over lath, about ⅞ in total (R703.7).</li>\n<li>Weep screed at the foundation plate line: at least 4 in above earth or 2 in above paved areas (R703.7.2.1).</li>\n<li>Two layers of Grade D paper over wood-based sheathing (R703.7.3).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Below grade</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dampproofing: foundation walls that retain earth and enclose interior spaces below grade (R406.1).</li>\n<li>Waterproofing: where there's a high water table or severe soil-water conditions (R406.2).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Firestopping vs fireblocking</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Firestopping: rated penetration and joint systems in fire-resistance-rated assemblies (IBC 714, 715).</li>\n<li>Fireblocking: blocking concealed draft openings in combustible construction (IBC 718; IRC R302.11).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>OSHA:</strong> a low-slope roof is 4:12 or less (1926.500(b)).</p>","qcount":15},{"id":"07-doors-windows","n":7,"name":"Doors & Windows","items":5,"books":"in-room","blurb":"Egress, safety glazing, flashing and fall protection at openings.","ref":"<p>Main sources: IRC R308 (safety glazing), R310 (emergency escape), R311 (egress doors), R312.2 (window fall protection), R302.5 (garage doors), Ch. 6 (headers, installation); IBC 1010 (doors), 2406 (safety glazing). Background: <em>Carpentry and Building Construction</em> (installation).</p>","html":"<h2>Key concepts</h2>\n<p><strong>Emergency escape and rescue openings (IRC R310)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Requirement</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Net clear opening</td>\n<td>5.7 sq ft (5.0 sq ft at grade floor or below grade)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Min clear height</td>\n<td>24 in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Min clear width</td>\n<td>20 in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max sill height</td>\n<td>44 in above floor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Where required</td>\n<td>Basements, habitable attics, every sleeping room</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Window wells</td>\n<td>9 sq ft min area, 36 in min projection; ladder if deeper than 44 in</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Egress doors</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>IRC: at least one egress door, 32 in clear width, 78 in high, side-hinged (R311.2).</li>\n<li>IBC: 32 in clear width, 80 in high (1010.1.1).</li>\n<li>IBC: doors must swing in the direction of egress travel for an occupant load of 50 or more, or in Group H (1010.1.2.1).</li>\n<li>IBC: panic or fire exit hardware is required in Group A and E with an occupant load of 50 or more (1010.1.10).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Safety glazing: hazardous locations (IRC R308.4 / IBC 2406.4)</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Glazing in all doors (fixed or operable panels).</li>\n<li>Within a 24 in arc of either vertical door edge, in the closed position, with the bottom edge less than 60 in above the floor.</li>\n<li>Large individual panes: all four conditions must apply. Area over 9 sq ft; bottom edge less than 18 in above the floor; top edge more than 36 in above the floor; a walking surface within 36 in horizontally.</li>\n<li>Guards and railings.</li>\n<li>Wet areas: walls and enclosures of tubs, showers, hot tubs and saunas, where the bottom edge is less than 60 in above the standing surface.</li>\n<li>Near pools and spas.</li>\n<li>Adjacent to stairs and ramps: bottom edge less than 36 in above the walking surface.</li>\n<li>At the bottom of a stair landing: within 60 in horizontally of the bottom tread, with the bottom edge less than 36 in above the walking surface.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Window fall protection (IRC R312.2).</strong> Where the opening is more than 72 in above finished grade, the sill must be at least 24 in above the floor, or the window needs an opening-limiting device or fall prevention device (ASTM F2090).</p>\n<p><strong>Garage-to-dwelling door (IRC R302.5.1).</strong> Solid wood or solid- or honeycomb-core steel at least 1⅜ in thick, or a 20-minute fire-rated door. No openings are allowed from a garage directly into a sleeping room. Check R302.5.1 in your book for whether a self-closing device is required.</p>\n<p><strong>Fenestration energy.</strong> NFRC labels show U-factor and SHGC. Zone 2: U-0.40, SHGC 0.25.</p>\n<p><strong>Installation</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Apply the sill pan or flashing first, then the jambs, then the head flashing on top, lapped over the WRB, all shingle-fashion.</li>\n<li>Rough opening is typically the unit size plus about ½ in each way.</li>\n<li>Headers carry the load over the opening, with jack (trimmer) studs and king studs at each side.</li>\n</ul>","qcount":12},{"id":"08-finishes","n":8,"name":"Finishes","items":6,"books":"in-room","blurb":"Gypsum board, plaster, flooring and interior finishes.","ref":"<p>Main sources: IRC R702 (interior covering), R703.7 (stucco), R302.6 (garage separation), R302.9 (flame spread); IBC Ch. 8 (interior finishes), Ch. 25 (gypsum and plaster). Background: <em>Carpentry and Building Construction</em> (drywall, trim, flooring, paint).</p>","html":"<h2>Key concepts</h2>\n<p><strong>Gypsum board</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Regular: ½ in.</li>\n<li>Type X: ⅝ in, fire-rated.</li>\n<li>Moisture-resistant (green board): not allowed as a tile backer in showers or tubs under the 2018 IRC (R702.3.7).</li>\n<li>Shower and tub walls: use cement, fiber-cement, glass-mat gypsum or fiber-reinforced gypsum backer (R702.4.2).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Fastener spacing (IRC Table R702.3.5), framing at 16 in o.c.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Location</th>\n<th>Nails</th>\n<th>Screws</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ceilings</td>\n<td>7 in o.c.</td>\n<td>12 in o.c.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Walls</td>\n<td>8 in o.c.</td>\n<td>16 in o.c.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Finish levels (GA-214)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Level 0: no taping.</li>\n<li>Level 1: tape set in compound, for plenums and above ceilings.</li>\n<li>Level 2: one coat over tape, for garages and tile substrate.</li>\n<li>Level 3: heavy texture.</li>\n<li>Level 4: flat paint and light texture (the most common).</li>\n<li>Level 5: skim coat over the whole surface, for gloss paint or critical lighting.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Garage separation (IRC R302.6)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>½ in gypsum board on the garage side of walls and ceilings.</li>\n<li>⅝ in Type X on the ceiling when habitable rooms are above.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Flame spread (IBC 803.1.2, ASTM E84)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Class</th>\n<th>Flame spread index</th>\n<th>Smoke-developed</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>0–25</td>\n<td>450 or less</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>26–75</td>\n<td>450 or less</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>76–200</td>\n<td>450 or less</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>IRC R302.9: wall and ceiling finishes max flame spread 200, smoke-developed 450.</p>\n<p><strong>Plaster and stucco</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Three-coat work: scratch coat, then brown coat, then finish coat.</li>\n<li>Exterior Portland cement plaster (stucco): about ⅞ in total over lath (R703.7). Moist-cure the base coats.</li>\n<li>Interior gypsum plaster: R702.2.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tile.</strong> Thinset mortar over cement backer; or a mortar bed (mud set). Allow for movement joints.</p>\n<p><strong>Paint.</strong> Prep is everything: clean, dry, sound surface. Primer seals and gives adhesion. Latex is water-based with fast dry and low odor; alkyd/oil is solvent-based. Apply within the manufacturer's temperature and humidity ranges.</p>\n<p><strong>Wood flooring.</strong> Acclimate it on site. Leave an expansion gap at the walls (usually about ½–¾ in), hidden by base or shoe.</p>","qcount":10},{"id":"09-safety","n":9,"name":"Safety","items":12,"books":"in-room","blurb":"OSHA 1926 numbers you must be able to find fast.","ref":"<p>Safety comes straight from the OSHA 1926 book you bring in, so these are mostly lookup points. Tab the subparts below and drill finding the numbers fast.</p>","html":"<h2>Tab map (29 CFR 1926)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tab</th>\n<th>Subpart</th>\n<th>Sections</th>\n<th>What lives there</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>General</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>1926.20–.35</td>\n<td>Competent person, training, first aid, emergency action</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Health</td>\n<td>D</td>\n<td>1926.50–.66</td>\n<td>First aid, noise (.52), HazCom (.59), silica (.1153 is in Z)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PPE</td>\n<td>E</td>\n<td>1926.95–.107</td>\n<td>Hard hats, eye/face, hearing, respirators</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fire</td>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>1926.150–.159</td>\n<td>Extinguishers, flammable liquids</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Signs</td>\n<td>G</td>\n<td>1926.200–.203</td>\n<td>Signs, signals, barricades</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Materials</td>\n<td>H</td>\n<td>1926.250–.252</td>\n<td>Stacking, rigging, waste disposal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tools</td>\n<td>I</td>\n<td>1926.300–.307</td>\n<td>Guards, powder-actuated tools</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Welding</td>\n<td>J</td>\n<td>1926.350–.354</td>\n<td>Gas cylinders, arc welding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electrical</td>\n<td>K</td>\n<td>1926.400–.449</td>\n<td>GFCI / assured grounding (.404)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scaffolds</td>\n<td>L</td>\n<td>1926.450–.454</td>\n<td>Capacity, planking, guardrails, fall protection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fall Protection</td>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>1926.500–.503</td>\n<td>Triggers (.501), systems (.502), training (.503)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cranes</td>\n<td>CC</td>\n<td>1926.1400–.1442</td>\n<td>Power line clearance (.1408), signals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Excavations</td>\n<td>P</td>\n<td>1926.650–.652 + App. A/B</td>\n<td>Soil types, slopes, egress, inspections</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Concrete &amp; Masonry</td>\n<td>Q</td>\n<td>1926.700–.706</td>\n<td>Rebar caps, formwork, limited access zone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Steel Erection</td>\n<td>R</td>\n<td>1926.750–.761</td>\n<td>Anchor rods, 2-bolt rule, joists, decking, fall protection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Demolition</td>\n<td>T</td>\n<td>1926.850–.860</td>\n<td>Engineering survey</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stairs &amp; Ladders</td>\n<td>X</td>\n<td>1926.1050–.1060</td>\n<td>Stair triggers, ladder extension, angles</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>Key numbers to know cold</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Rule</th>\n<th>Number</th>\n<th>Cite</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>General fall protection trigger</td>\n<td>6 ft</td>\n<td>1926.501(b)(1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scaffold fall protection trigger</td>\n<td>10 ft</td>\n<td>1926.451(g)(1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Steel erection fall protection (non-connectors)</td>\n<td>15 ft</td>\n<td>1926.760(a)(1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Steel connectors</td>\n<td>2 stories or 30 ft</td>\n<td>1926.760(b)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Controlled decking zone</td>\n<td>15 to 30 ft</td>\n<td>1926.760(c)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Guardrail top rail height</td>\n<td>42 in ± 3 in</td>\n<td>1926.502(b)(1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Top rail strength</td>\n<td>200 lb outward/downward</td>\n<td>1926.502(b)(3)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Midrail strength</td>\n<td>150 lb</td>\n<td>1926.502(b)(5)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Toeboard height</td>\n<td>3½ in min</td>\n<td>1926.502(j)(3)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PFAS anchorage</td>\n<td>5,000 lb per worker (or 2× safety factor, designed)</td>\n<td>1926.502(d)(15)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PFAS free fall</td>\n<td>6 ft max</td>\n<td>1926.502(d)(16)(iii)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PFAS deceleration distance</td>\n<td>3½ ft max</td>\n<td>1926.502(d)(16)(iv)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hole covers</td>\n<td>Support 2× the load; marked \"HOLE\" or \"COVER\"</td>\n<td>1926.502(i)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Low-slope roof</td>\n<td>4 in 12 or less</td>\n<td>1926.500(b)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Warning line from roof edge</td>\n<td>6 ft min</td>\n<td>1926.502(f)(1)(i)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scaffold capacity</td>\n<td>Own weight + 4× max intended load</td>\n<td>1926.451(a)(1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scaffold plank overhang</td>\n<td>6 in min, 12 in max (≤10 ft planks)</td>\n<td>1926.451(b)(4)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scaffold height-to-base &gt; 4:1</td>\n<td>Must be guyed/tied/braced</td>\n<td>1926.451(c)(1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stairway or ladder required</td>\n<td>Break in elevation of 19 in or more</td>\n<td>1926.1051(a)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stair rails required</td>\n<td>4+ risers or rise &gt; 30 in</td>\n<td>1926.1052(c)(1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ladder side rails above landing</td>\n<td>3 ft</td>\n<td>1926.1053(b)(1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Portable ladder angle</td>\n<td>1 ft out per 4 ft of working length</td>\n<td>1926.1053(b)(5)(i)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rung spacing</td>\n<td>10 to 14 in</td>\n<td>1926.1053(a)(3)(i)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Excavation protective system</td>\n<td>5 ft or deeper (unless stable rock)</td>\n<td>1926.652(a)(1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PE design of protective system</td>\n<td>Deeper than 20 ft</td>\n<td>1926.652(b)/(c)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Trench egress</td>\n<td>At 4 ft deep; within 25 ft lateral travel</td>\n<td>1926.651(c)(2)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spoil / equipment setback</td>\n<td>2 ft from edge min</td>\n<td>1926.651(j)(2)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Daily excavation inspection</td>\n<td>Competent person, before each shift</td>\n<td>1926.651(k)(1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Type A soil</td>\n<td>≥ 1.5 tsf unconfined compressive strength</td>\n<td>App. A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Type B soil</td>\n<td>0.5 to 1.5 tsf</td>\n<td>App. A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Type C soil</td>\n<td>≤ 0.5 tsf</td>\n<td>App. A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max slope Type A / B / C</td>\n<td>¾:1 (53°) / 1:1 (45°) / 1½:1 (34°)</td>\n<td>App. B, Table B-1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Masonry limited access zone</td>\n<td>Wall height + 4 ft wide, full wall length</td>\n<td>1926.706(a)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Steel column anchorage</td>\n<td>4 anchor rods min</td>\n<td>1926.755(a)(1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Solid-web members before releasing line</td>\n<td>2 bolts per connection, wrench-tight</td>\n<td>1926.756(a)(1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GFCI on temporary power</td>\n<td>All 120 V, 1-phase, 15 and 20 A receptacles (or assured grounding program)</td>\n<td>1926.404(b)(1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fire extinguisher</td>\n<td>2A per 3,000 sq ft; ≤100 ft travel</td>\n<td>1926.150(c)(1)(i)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Crane power line clearance (≤350 kV)</td>\n<td>20 ft</td>\n<td>1926.1408</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Noise</td>\n<td>90 dBA, 8-hr permissible exposure</td>\n<td>1926.52, Table D-2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>","qcount":22},{"id":"10-general-building-code","n":10,"name":"General Building Code","items":9,"books":"in-room","blurb":"IBC and IRC tab maps, occupancy, construction types and egress.","ref":"<p>These are pure lookup points, so tab well. On a dual (KB-2) license, expect both IBC and IRC questions.</p>","html":"<h2>Tab map: 2018 IBC</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tab</th>\n<th>Chapter</th>\n<th>Look here for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Admin</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Permits (105), exempt work (105.2), permit expiration (105.5), inspections (110), certificate of occupancy (111), stop work (115)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Definitions</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Exact meanings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Occupancy</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Groups A, B, E, F, H, I, M, R, S, U</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Height and Area</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Tables 504.3, 504.4, 506.2; mixed occupancy (508)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Types of Construction</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Types I–V; Table 601 (element ratings), Table 602 (exterior walls)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fire-Resistance</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Fire walls (706), fire barriers (707), penetrations (714), fireblocking (718)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interior Finishes</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Flame spread classes (803)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fire Protection</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Sprinklers (903), alarms (907)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Egress</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Occupant load Table 1004.5, doors (1010), stairs (1011), handrails (1014), guards (1015), travel distance (1017), corridors (1020)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Accessibility</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Parking Table 1106.1, accessible routes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interior Environment</td>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Ventilation, ceiling heights, room sizes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Roofs</td>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>Roof coverings (1507)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Structural</td>\n<td>16</td>\n<td>Risk category Table 1604.5, live loads Table 1607.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Special Inspections</td>\n<td>17</td>\n<td>1704 (who), 1705 (what)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Soils and Foundations</td>\n<td>18</td>\n<td>Presumptive bearing Table 1806.2; footing depth 1809.4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Concrete / Masonry / Steel / Wood</td>\n<td>19 / 21 / 22 / 23</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Glass</td>\n<td>24</td>\n<td>Safety glazing 2406</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gypsum</td>\n<td>25</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Safeguards During Construction</td>\n<td>33</td>\n<td>Pedestrian protection Table 3306.1; standpipes 3311</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>Tab map: 2018 IRC</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tab</th>\n<th>Section</th>\n<th>Look here for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Design Criteria</td>\n<td>R301</td>\n<td>Table R301.2(1) climate; live loads Table R301.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fire-Resistant Construction</td>\n<td>R302</td>\n<td>Garage separation R302.6, fireblocking R302.11</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Light, Ventilation</td>\n<td>R303</td>\n<td>8% glazing, 4% openable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Room Area / Ceiling Height</td>\n<td>R304 / R305</td>\n<td>70 sq ft, 7 ft dimension / 7 ft ceilings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Safety Glazing</td>\n<td>R308</td>\n<td>Hazardous locations R308.4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Emergency Escape</td>\n<td>R310</td>\n<td>5.7 sq ft, 24 in, 20 in, 44 in sill</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Means of Egress</td>\n<td>R311</td>\n<td>Door R311.2, stairs R311.7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Guards</td>\n<td>R312</td>\n<td>36 in, 4 in sphere, window sills R312.2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Smoke / CO Alarms</td>\n<td>R314 / R315</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Foundations</td>\n<td>R401–R408</td>\n<td>Table R403.1, R403.1.6 anchor bolts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Floors / Walls / Roofs</td>\n<td>Ch. 5 / 6 / 8</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wall Covering</td>\n<td>Ch. 7</td>\n<td>WRB, flashing, stucco, veneer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Roof Coverings</td>\n<td>Ch. 9</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Energy</td>\n<td>Ch. 11 (N1101+)</td>\n<td>Table N1102.1.2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>Key numbers</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>IBC</th>\n<th>IRC</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Guard height</td>\n<td>42 in (1015.3)</td>\n<td>36 in (R312.1.2)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Guard trigger</td>\n<td>Drop of more than 30 in</td>\n<td>Drop of more than 30 in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Guard openings</td>\n<td>4 in sphere (1015.4)</td>\n<td>4 in sphere (R312.1.3)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Handrail height</td>\n<td>34–38 in (1014.2)</td>\n<td>34–38 in (R311.7.8.1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stair riser</td>\n<td>Max 7 in, min 4 in (1011.5.2)</td>\n<td>Max 7¾ in (R311.7.5.1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stair tread</td>\n<td>Min 11 in</td>\n<td>Min 10 in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stair headroom</td>\n<td>80 in (1011.3)</td>\n<td>6 ft 8 in (R311.7.2)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stair width</td>\n<td>44 in (36 in if occupant load under 50) (1011.2)</td>\n<td>36 in (R311.7.1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Egress door</td>\n<td>32 in clear, 80 in high (1010.1.1)</td>\n<td>32 in clear, 78 in high (R311.2)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Corridor width</td>\n<td>44 in (36 in if occupant load under 50) (1020.2)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Footing depth</td>\n<td>12 in below undisturbed ground (1809.4)</td>\n<td>12 in (R403.1.4)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Live load, dwelling</td>\n<td>40 psf (Table 1607.1)</td>\n<td>40 psf living, 30 psf sleeping (Table R301.5)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Live load, offices</td>\n<td>50 psf</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Presumptive load-bearing values (IBC Table 1806.2 / IRC Table R401.4.1)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Soil</th>\n<th>psf</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Crystalline bedrock</td>\n<td>12,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sedimentary rock</td>\n<td>4,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sandy gravel and gravel</td>\n<td>3,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sand, silty sand, clayey sand, silty gravel, clayey gravel</td>\n<td>2,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clay, sandy clay, silty clay, clayey silt</td>\n<td>1,500</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Occupancy quick list</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Group</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>Assembly, 50 or more occupants (A-1 theaters, A-2 restaurants, A-3 churches and gyms)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>Business: offices, banks, outpatient clinics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E</td>\n<td>Educational through 12th grade, 6 or more people</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F</td>\n<td>Factory (F-1 moderate, F-2 low hazard)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>H</td>\n<td>High hazard</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I</td>\n<td>Institutional (hospitals, jails, assisted living)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>M</td>\n<td>Mercantile (retail)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>R</td>\n<td>R-1 transient (hotels), R-2 three or more dwelling units (apartments), R-3, R-4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>S</td>\n<td>Storage (S-1 moderate, S-2 low)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>U</td>\n<td>Utility and miscellaneous (sheds, carports, fences)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Construction types</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Types I and II: noncombustible.</li>\n<li>Type III: noncombustible exterior walls, interior of any material.</li>\n<li>Type IV: heavy timber.</li>\n<li>Type V: any material the code allows.</li>\n<li>\"A\" means protected (rated); \"B\" means unprotected.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Admin</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A permit expires if work doesn't start within <strong>180 days</strong> or is suspended for 180 days (105.5).</li>\n<li>The special inspector is employed by the <strong>owner or owner's agent</strong>, not the contractor (1704.2).</li>\n<li>During construction, a standpipe is required before the building exceeds <strong>40 ft</strong> above the lowest fire department access (3311.1).</li>\n</ul>","qcount":17}],"questions":[{"id":"X1","d":"01-sitework","q":"In Arizona, before excavating you must notify Arizona 811 (Blue Stake) at least:","o":["24 hours in advance","2 full working days in advance","5 working days in advance","10 calendar days in advance"],"a":1,"e":"Two full working days","r":"A.R.S. § 40-360.22"},{"id":"X2","d":"01-sitework","q":"100 bank cubic yards of common earth with a 25% swell factor will produce about how many loose cubic yards?","o":["75","100","125","133"],"a":2,"e":"100 × 1.25 = 125 loose cu yd","r":""},{"id":"X3","d":"01-sitework","q":"The laboratory test used to set the maximum dry density and optimum moisture content for compaction specs is the:","o":["Slump test","Proctor test","Atterberg limits test","Sieve analysis"],"a":1,"e":"Proctor (standard ASTM D698 or modified ASTM D1557)","r":""},{"id":"X4","d":"01-sitework","q":"The best compactor for cohesive clay soils is a:","o":["Smooth-drum vibratory roller","Vibratory plate","Sheepsfoot (padfoot) roller","Rubber-tired loader"],"a":2,"e":"Sheepsfoot. It kneads the clay","r":""},{"id":"X5","d":"01-sitework","q":"A benchmark elevation is 100.00 ft. The backsight on it reads 5.20 ft, and the foresight on a stake reads 3.70 ft. What is the stake elevation?","o":["96.30 ft","98.50 ft","101.50 ft","108.90 ft"],"a":2,"e":"HI = 100.00 + 5.20 = 105.20; 105.20 − 3.70 = 101.50 ft","r":""},{"id":"X6","d":"01-sitework","q":"Using the average end area method, two cross sections 100 ft apart have areas of 60 sq ft and 48 sq ft. The volume is:","o":["200 cu yd","400 cu yd","5,400 cu yd","10,800 cu yd"],"a":0,"e":"(60 + 48) ÷ 2 × 100 = 5,400 cu ft ÷ 27 = 200 cu yd","r":""},{"id":"X7","d":"01-sitework","q":"Under the IRC, the lot must be graded so the ground falls at least ___ within the first 10 ft from the foundation.","o":["2 in","4 in","6 in","12 in"],"a":2,"e":"6 in (5%)","r":"IRC R401.3"},{"id":"X8","d":"01-sitework","q":"For flexible pipe such as PVC, the most critical zone of the pipe embedment for structural support is the:","o":["Final backfill","Haunching","Surface restoration","Trench wall"],"a":1,"e":"Haunching (from the bedding to the springline)","r":"Pipe and Excavation Contracting"},{"id":"X9","d":"01-sitework","q":"Thrust blocks are installed on pressurized water lines primarily at:","o":["Straight runs every 100 ft","Bends, tees, caps and valves","Service taps only","Manholes"],"a":1,"e":"","r":"Pipe and Excavation Contracting"},{"id":"X10","d":"01-sitework","q":"The minimum slope for a 4-in building sewer is:","o":["1/16 in per ft","⅛ in per ft","¼ in per ft","½ in per ft"],"a":1,"e":"⅛ in per ft for 3 to 6 in pipe (¼ in per ft for 2½ in and smaller)","r":"IRC P3005.3 / IPC 704.1"},{"id":"X11","d":"01-sitework","q":"An AZPDES Construction General Permit (with SWPPP) from ADEQ is required for construction that disturbs at least:","o":["¼ acre","½ acre","1 acre","5 acres"],"a":2,"e":"1 acre (or smaller sites that are part of a larger common plan)","r":""},{"id":"X12","d":"01-sitework","q":"Where the IBC grading appendix applies, cut and fill slopes generally may not be steeper than:","o":["1H:1V","1½H:1V","2H:1V","3H:1V"],"a":2,"e":"2 horizontal to 1 vertical","r":"IBC Appendix J (J106, J107)"},{"id":"X13","d":"01-sitework","q":"A wellpoint dewatering system works best in:","o":["Dense clay","Solid rock","Granular soils such as sand and gravel","Organic peat"],"a":2,"e":"","r":"Pipe and Excavation Contracting"},{"id":"C1","d":"02-concrete","q":"Which ASTM C150 cement type is specified for high early strength?","o":["Type I","Type II","Type III","Type V"],"a":2,"e":"Type III","r":"PCA Ch. 2 (Portland cements)"},{"id":"C2","d":"02-concrete","q":"Concrete exposed to soils with high sulfate content should use:","o":["Type I cement","Type III cement","Type IV cement","Type V cement"],"a":3,"e":"Type V (high sulfate resistance)","r":"PCA Ch. 2"},{"id":"C3","d":"02-concrete","q":"Increasing the water-cement ratio of a mix will:","o":["Increase strength and reduce permeability","Decrease strength and increase permeability","Have no effect on strength","Reduce shrinkage"],"a":1,"e":"More water means lower strength and higher permeability","r":"PCA Ch. 9 (Designing and proportioning)"},{"id":"C4","d":"02-concrete","q":"In the standard slump test, the cone is filled in ___ layers, each rodded ___ times.","o":["2 layers, 20 times","3 layers, 25 times","4 layers, 25 times","3 layers, 35 times"],"a":1,"e":"3 layers, 25 strokes each","r":"ASTM C143 / PCA Ch. 16 (Control tests)"},{"id":"C5","d":"02-concrete","q":"The standard age for testing concrete cylinders to verify specified compressive strength (f'c) is:","o":["3 days","7 days","14 days","28 days"],"a":3,"e":"28 days","r":"ACI 318 / PCA Ch. 16"},{"id":"C6","d":"02-concrete","q":"The primary purpose of air entrainment is to:","o":["Increase compressive strength","Improve resistance to freeze-thaw damage","Accelerate setting time","Reduce the amount of cement"],"a":1,"e":"Freeze-thaw durability (it also improves workability)","r":"PCA Ch. 8 (Air-entrained concrete)"},{"id":"C7","d":"02-concrete","q":"Calcium chloride is an admixture that:","o":["Retards set and is preferred for prestressed concrete","Accelerates set but can promote corrosion of embedded steel","Entrains air","Reduces water demand by 12–30%"],"a":1,"e":"An accelerator. It increases the risk of corrosion of reinforcing steel and is not used in prestressed concrete","r":"PCA Ch. 6 (Admixtures)"},{"id":"C8","d":"02-concrete","q":"Under ACI 318, the minimum concrete cover for reinforcement in concrete cast against and permanently exposed to earth is:","o":["¾ in","1½ in","2 in","3 in"],"a":3,"e":"3 in","r":"ACI 318 cover table, referenced by IBC 1901.2"},{"id":"C9","d":"02-concrete","q":"A #5 reinforcing bar has a nominal diameter of:","o":["½ in","⅝ in","¾ in","5/16 in"],"a":1,"e":"⅝ in (bar number = eighths of an inch)","r":"PCA / ASTM A615"},{"id":"C10","d":"02-concrete","q":"How many cubic yards of concrete are needed for a 30 ft × 40 ft slab 4 in thick (no waste)?","o":["11.1 cu yd","14.8 cu yd","16.0 cu yd","44.4 cu yd"],"a":1,"e":"30 × 40 × 0.333 ft = 400 cu ft ÷ 27 = 14.8 cu yd","r":""},{"id":"C11","d":"02-concrete","q":"The nominal maximum size of coarse aggregate should not exceed:","o":["1/3 the narrowest form dimension","1/5 the narrowest form dimension, 1/3 the slab depth, or 3/4 the clear spacing between bars","1/2 the slab depth","The clear cover to reinforcement"],"a":1,"e":"","r":"PCA Ch. 5 (Aggregates) / ACI 318"},{"id":"C12","d":"02-concrete","q":"Control joints in a slab on grade should be cut to a depth of about:","o":["1/8 of the slab thickness","1/4 of the slab thickness","1/2 of the slab thickness","The full slab thickness"],"a":1,"e":"1/4 of the slab thickness","r":"PCA Ch. 13 (Placing and finishing) / ACI 302"},{"id":"C13","d":"02-concrete","q":"Final finishing (floating and troweling) of a slab should begin:","o":["Immediately after screeding","While bleed water is still on the surface","After the bleed water has evaporated from the surface","After 24 hours"],"a":2,"e":"Working bleed water into the surface causes dusting, crazing and scaling","r":"PCA Ch. 13"},{"id":"C14","d":"02-concrete","q":"Cold-weather concreting procedures (ACI 306) apply when the air temperature has fallen to, or is expected to fall below:","o":["32°F","40°F","50°F","55°F"],"a":1,"e":"40°F during the protection period","r":"PCA Ch. 15 (Cold-weather concreting)"},{"id":"C15","d":"02-concrete","q":"Under the IRC, the minimum thickness of a concrete floor slab on ground is:","o":["3 in","3½ in","4 in","5 in"],"a":1,"e":"3½ in","r":"IRC R506.1"},{"id":"C16","d":"02-concrete","q":"The IRC requires a vapor retarder under a slab on ground over habitable space. The typical minimum is:","o":["2-mil polyethylene","4-mil polyethylene","6-mil polyethylene","15-lb felt"],"a":2,"e":"6-mil (0.006 in) polyethylene or approved equal","r":"IRC R506.2.3"},{"id":"C17","d":"02-concrete","q":"Fresh concrete samples for testing should be taken from:","o":["The first concrete discharged from the truck","The last concrete in the drum","The middle portion of the batch","Any point; it doesn't matter"],"a":2,"e":"The middle portion of the batch (ASTM C172)","r":"PCA Ch. 16"},{"id":"C18","d":"02-concrete","q":"Under OSHA, protruding reinforcing steel onto or into which employees could fall must be:","o":["Painted orange","Guarded to eliminate the hazard of impalement","Bent over at 90°","Removed before the next shift"],"a":1,"e":"Guarded (for example, with rebar caps or troughs designed for impalement protection)","r":"1926.701(b)"},{"id":"Y1","d":"03-masonry","q":"Which mortar type has the highest compressive strength?","o":["Type M","Type S","Type N","Type O"],"a":0,"e":"Type M (about 2,500 psi)","r":"ASTM C270 / Modern Masonry"},{"id":"Y2","d":"03-masonry","q":"The mortar type most commonly recommended for general exterior, above-grade, non-structural walls and brick veneer is:","o":["Type M","Type S","Type N","Type K"],"a":2,"e":"Type N","r":"ASTM C270 / Modern Masonry"},{"id":"Y3","d":"03-masonry","q":"The actual dimensions of a nominal 8 × 8 × 16 CMU are:","o":["8 × 8 × 16 in","7⅝ × 7⅝ × 15⅝ in","7½ × 7½ × 15½ in","7¾ × 7¾ × 15¾ in"],"a":1,"e":"Nominal size minus the ⅜ in mortar joint","r":""},{"id":"Y4","d":"03-masonry","q":"About how many 8 × 16 in nominal CMUs are needed per 100 sq ft of wall?","o":["90","100","112.5","125"],"a":2,"e":"144 ÷ (8 × 16 = 128) = 1.125 per sq ft, so 112.5 per 100 sq ft","r":""},{"id":"Y5","d":"03-masonry","q":"About how many modular brick are needed per square foot of single-wythe wall with ⅜ in joints?","o":["4.5","5.5","6.75","7.5"],"a":2,"e":"About 6.75 (675 per 100 sq ft)","r":""},{"id":"Y6","d":"03-masonry","q":"A brick laid vertically on end with its narrow (stretcher) face exposed is called a:","o":["Rowlock","Sailor","Soldier","Header"],"a":2,"e":"Soldier","r":"Modern Masonry (brick positions)"},{"id":"Y7","d":"03-masonry","q":"A brick laid on its edge with the end exposed, often used for sills and wall caps, is a:","o":["Rowlock","Shiner","Soldier","Stretcher"],"a":0,"e":"Rowlock","r":""},{"id":"Y8","d":"03-masonry","q":"Which mortar joint profiles give the best weather resistance?","o":["Raked and struck","Concave and V","Flush and weathered","Extruded and beaded"],"a":1,"e":"Concave and V. Tooling compresses the mortar and sheds water","r":"Modern Masonry"},{"id":"Y9","d":"03-masonry","q":"Mortar should be used and placed in its final position within ___ of initial mixing.","o":["1 hour","1½ hours","2½ hours","4 hours"],"a":2,"e":"2½ hours","r":"TMS 602 / Modern Masonry"},{"id":"Y10","d":"03-masonry","q":"Efflorescence on a brick wall is caused by:","o":["Excess Portland cement in the mortar","Water-soluble salts carried to the surface by moisture","Freeze-thaw cycling","Too many weep holes"],"a":1,"e":"","r":"Modern Masonry"},{"id":"Y11","d":"03-masonry","q":"In anchored masonry veneer, weep holes must be spaced no more than:","o":["24 in o.c.","32 in o.c.","33 in o.c.","48 in o.c."],"a":2,"e":"33 in o.c., at least 3/16 in diameter, immediately above the flashing","r":"IRC R703.8.6"},{"id":"Y12","d":"03-masonry","q":"Each veneer tie may support no more than ___ of wall area.","o":["2.0 sq ft","2.67 sq ft","3.5 sq ft","4.5 sq ft"],"a":1,"e":"2.67 sq ft (max 32 in horizontal and 24 in vertical spacing)","r":"IRC R703.8.4.1"},{"id":"Y13","d":"03-masonry","q":"Which statement about movement joints is correct?","o":["Brick shrinks, so it gets control joints","CMU expands, so it gets expansion joints","Brick expands, so it gets expansion joints; CMU shrinks, so it gets control joints","Neither material needs movement joints if it is reinforced"],"a":2,"e":"","r":"Modern Masonry"},{"id":"Y14","d":"03-masonry","q":"Masonry laid in stack bond requires:","o":["Type O mortar","Header courses every sixth course","Horizontal joint reinforcement","Weep holes in every head joint"],"a":2,"e":"Stack bond has no overlap, so joint reinforcement is needed for continuity","r":""},{"id":"Y15","d":"03-masonry","q":"Cold-weather masonry construction procedures are required when the ambient temperature falls below:","o":["32°F","40°F","45°F","50°F"],"a":1,"e":"40°F","r":"TMS 602 / IBC 2104"},{"id":"Y16","d":"03-masonry","q":"Grout for masonry is specified by:","o":["ASTM C270","ASTM C476","ASTM C90","ASTM C150"],"a":1,"e":"ASTM C476. (C270 = mortar, C90 = load-bearing CMU, C150 = Portland cement.)","r":""},{"id":"M1","d":"04-metals","q":"LH-series steel joists are:","o":["Light-gauge cold-formed joists","Longspan open-web joists","Composite joists","Joist girders"],"a":1,"e":"Longspan","r":"SJI"},{"id":"M2","d":"04-metals","q":"The minimum bearing length for a K-series joist on a steel member is:","o":["1½ in","2½ in","4 in","6 in"],"a":1,"e":"2½ in","r":"SJI K-series specification"},{"id":"M3","d":"04-metals","q":"The minimum bearing length for a K-series joist on masonry or concrete is:","o":["2½ in","3 in","4 in","6 in"],"a":2,"e":"4 in","r":"SJI K-series specification"},{"id":"M4","d":"04-metals","q":"LH- and DLH-series joists bearing on masonry or concrete require a minimum bearing of:","o":["2½ in","4 in","6 in","8 in"],"a":2,"e":"6 in (4 in on steel)","r":"SJI LH/DLH specification"},{"id":"M5","d":"04-metals","q":"Steel joists should never be lifted or handled by:","o":["The top chord at panel points","Spreader bars","Their bridging","Choker slings at the ends"],"a":2,"e":"Bridging (or web members)","r":"SJI handling guide"},{"id":"M6","d":"04-metals","q":"Construction loads may be placed on steel joists:","o":["As soon as the joist is set on its seat","Only after bridging is installed and anchored and the joist bearing ends are attached","Only after the deck is welded","Once the hoisting line is released"],"a":1,"e":"","r":"1926.757(e); SJI"},{"id":"M7","d":"04-metals","q":"For joists that require bolted diagonal erection bridging, that bridging must be installed:","o":["Before the hoisting line is released","Before the end of the shift","Before decking is placed","Before the joists are welded"],"a":0,"e":"","r":"1926.757(c) and Tables A/B; SJI erection stability"},{"id":"M8","d":"04-metals","q":"Under OSHA, solid-web structural members may not be released from the hoisting line until each connection has at least:","o":["One bolt, wrench-tight","Two bolts, wrench-tight","Four bolts, fully pretensioned","A tack weld"],"a":1,"e":"Two bolts per connection, of the size and strength shown on the erection drawings, drawn up wrench-tight","r":"1926.756(a)(1)"},{"id":"M9","d":"04-metals","q":"Each column must be anchored with a minimum of:","o":["2 anchor rods","3 anchor rods","4 anchor rods","6 anchor rods"],"a":2,"e":"4","r":"1926.755(a)(1)"},{"id":"M10","d":"04-metals","q":"A controlled decking zone (CDZ) may be used for leading-edge decking work only at heights:","o":["Up to 15 ft","Over 15 ft and up to 30 ft","Up to 2 stories or 30 ft","Any height with a safety monitor"],"a":1,"e":"Over 15 ft up to 30 ft","r":"1926.760(c)"},{"id":"M11","d":"04-metals","q":"Connectors must be protected by fall protection when working at heights over:","o":["6 ft","15 ft","2 stories or 30 ft, whichever is less","4 stories or 50 ft"],"a":2,"e":"","r":"1926.760(b)"},{"id":"M12","d":"04-metals","q":"The ASTM specification most commonly used for wide-flange (W) shapes, with a 50 ksi yield strength, is:","o":["A36","A53","A500","A992"],"a":3,"e":"A992","r":"AISC"},{"id":"M13","d":"04-metals","q":"The governing code for structural steel welding is:","o":["AWS D1.1","ACI 318","ASTM A615","TMS 402"],"a":0,"e":"AWS D1.1 Structural Welding Code — Steel","r":"IBC 2204.1 / AISC 360"},{"id":"M14","d":"04-metals","q":"Before steel erection begins on footings, piers or walls, the concrete or mortar must have reached at least what percentage of its intended minimum compressive design strength (or have adequate strength verified)?","o":["50%","65%","75%","100%"],"a":2,"e":"75%","r":"1926.752(a)(1)"},{"id":"M15","d":"04-metals","q":"The main purpose of joist bridging is to:","o":["Carry the roof dead load","Hold the chords in position and prevent lateral buckling","Provide a nailing surface for decking","Increase the joist's vertical load capacity rating"],"a":1,"e":"","r":"SJI"},{"id":"W1","d":"05-carpentry","q":"The actual dimensions of a nominal 2×10 are:","o":["2 × 10 in","1¾ × 9½ in","1½ × 9¼ in","1½ × 9½ in"],"a":2,"e":"1½ × 9¼ in","r":""},{"id":"W2","d":"05-carpentry","q":"How many board feet are in one 2×6 that is 12 ft long?","o":["6","12","14","24"],"a":1,"e":"(2 × 6 × 12) ÷ 12 = 12 bd ft","r":""},{"id":"W3","d":"05-carpentry","q":"A 16d common nail is how long?","o":["2½ in","3 in","3¼ in","3½ in"],"a":3,"e":"3½ in","r":""},{"id":"W4","d":"05-carpentry","q":"Under the IRC, foundation anchor bolts must be at least ½ in diameter, embedded at least 7 in, and spaced not more than:","o":["4 ft o.c.","6 ft o.c.","8 ft o.c.","10 ft o.c."],"a":1,"e":"6 ft o.c., with one bolt within 12 in of each end of each plate section","r":"IRC R403.1.6"},{"id":"W5","d":"05-carpentry","q":"In an exterior or bearing wall, a bored hole in a single stud may not exceed what percentage of the stud width?","o":["25%","33%","40%","60%"],"a":2,"e":"40% (up to 60% if the stud is doubled and no more than two successive studs are bored)","r":"IRC R602.6"},{"id":"W6","d":"05-carpentry","q":"In a bearing wall, a notch in a stud may not exceed:","o":["10% of the width","25% of the width","40% of the width","1/3 of the width"],"a":1,"e":"25%","r":"IRC R602.6"},{"id":"W7","d":"05-carpentry","q":"Notches in sawn-lumber floor joists may not exceed 1/6 of the joist depth and may not be located:","o":["Within 2 in of the top edge","In the middle third of the span","Within 12 in of a bearing","In the outer quarters of the span"],"a":1,"e":"The middle third of the span","r":"IRC R502.8.1"},{"id":"W8","d":"05-carpentry","q":"Bored holes in a floor joist may not be within ___ of the top or bottom edge, and may not exceed 1/3 the joist depth.","o":["1 in","1½ in","2 in","3 in"],"a":2,"e":"2 in","r":"IRC R502.8.1"},{"id":"W9","d":"05-carpentry","q":"End joints in a double top plate must be offset by at least:","o":["12 in","16 in","24 in","48 in"],"a":2,"e":"24 in","r":"IRC R602.3.2"},{"id":"W10","d":"05-carpentry","q":"In combustible construction, fireblocking is required in concealed wall spaces at the ceiling and floor levels and at intervals not exceeding:","o":["8 ft","10 ft","12 ft","16 ft"],"a":1,"e":"10 ft, both vertically and horizontally","r":"IRC R302.11"},{"id":"W11","d":"05-carpentry","q":"A structural panel stamped \"32/16\" may be used over roof framing spaced up to ___ and floor framing up to ___.","o":["16 in / 32 in","32 in / 16 in","24 in / 16 in","32 in / 24 in"],"a":1,"e":"32 in roof / 16 in floor","r":""},{"id":"W12","d":"05-carpentry","q":"In a crawl space, wood joists closer than ___ to exposed ground must be naturally durable or preservative-treated.","o":["8 in","12 in","18 in","24 in"],"a":2,"e":"18 in (12 in for girders/beams)","r":"IRC R317.1"},{"id":"W13","d":"05-carpentry","q":"Wood framing resting on an exterior foundation wall must be treated or naturally durable when it is less than ___ from exposed earth.","o":["6 in","8 in","12 in","18 in"],"a":1,"e":"8 in","r":"IRC R317.1"},{"id":"W14","d":"05-carpentry","q":"A roof has a 6-in-12 pitch and a common rafter run of 12 ft. What is the rafter's line length (no overhang)?","o":["12.0 ft","13.4 ft","14.2 ft","18.0 ft"],"a":1,"e":"Rise = 6 ft; √(12² + 6²) = √180 = 13.42 ft","r":""},{"id":"W15","d":"05-carpentry","q":"Collar ties in a rafter-framed roof are located:","o":["At the top plate","In the lower third of the attic space","At mid-height of the rafter","In the upper third of the attic space"],"a":3,"e":"","r":"IRC R802.4.6"},{"id":"W16","d":"05-carpentry","q":"A floor-to-floor rise of 108 in with a target riser of about 7½ in requires how many risers?","o":["13","14","15","16"],"a":2,"e":"108 ÷ 7.5 = 14.4, rounded up to 15 risers (7.2 in each, under the IRC 7¾ in max)","r":""},{"id":"W17","d":"05-carpentry","q":"Lumber grade-stamped \"S-DRY\" was surfaced at a moisture content of:","o":["12% or less","15% or less","19% or less","25% or less"],"a":2,"e":"19% or less","r":""},{"id":"W18","d":"05-carpentry","q":"Joist ends bearing on masonry or concrete need a minimum bearing of:","o":["1½ in","2 in","3 in","4 in"],"a":2,"e":"3 in (1½ in on wood or metal)","r":"IRC R502.6"},{"id":"T1","d":"06-thermal-moisture","q":"A Class I vapor retarder has a permeance of:","o":["0.1 perm or less","Over 0.1 up to 1.0 perm","Over 1.0 up to 10 perm","Over 10 perm"],"a":0,"e":"","r":"IRC R202 definitions / R702.7"},{"id":"T2","d":"06-thermal-moisture","q":"The minimum net free ventilating area for an enclosed attic is ___ of the area of the vented space, unless the conditions for a reduced ratio are met.","o":["1/100","1/150","1/300","1/500"],"a":1,"e":"1/150 (1/300 permitted with conditions)","r":"IRC R806.2"},{"id":"T3","d":"06-thermal-moisture","q":"Asphalt shingles may be installed on roof slopes down to:","o":["1:12","2:12","3:12","4:12"],"a":1,"e":"2:12. Slopes from 2:12 up to less than 4:12 require double underlayment","r":"IRC R905.2.2"},{"id":"T4","d":"06-thermal-moisture","q":"The minimum slope for clay or concrete roof tile is:","o":["2:12","2½:12","3:12","4:12"],"a":1,"e":"2½:12","r":"IRC R905.3.2"},{"id":"T5","d":"06-thermal-moisture","q":"A built-up roof (other than coal-tar) must have a design slope of at least:","o":["⅛ in per ft","¼ in per ft","½ in per ft","1 in per ft"],"a":1,"e":"¼ unit vertical in 12 (2%)","r":"IBC 1507.10.1"},{"id":"T6","d":"06-thermal-moisture","q":"Drip edge at eaves and gables of a shingle roof must overlap at joints by at least ___ and be fastened at no more than 12 in o.c.","o":["1 in","2 in","3 in","4 in"],"a":1,"e":"2 in","r":"IRC R905.2.8.5"},{"id":"T7","d":"06-thermal-moisture","q":"A cricket or saddle is required on the ridge side of a chimney when the chimney's dimension parallel to the ridge exceeds:","o":["18 in","24 in","30 in","36 in"],"a":2,"e":"30 in","r":"IRC R903.2.2"},{"id":"T8","d":"06-thermal-moisture","q":"A new roof covering may not be installed over an existing roof when the existing roof has:","o":["Any layer of wood shingles","One layer of asphalt shingles","Two or more applications of any type of roof covering","Underlayment that is more than 10 years old"],"a":2,"e":"","r":"IRC R908.3.1.1"},{"id":"T9","d":"06-thermal-moisture","q":"A stucco weep screed must be placed at least ___ above earth or 2 in above paved areas.","o":["2 in","4 in","6 in","8 in"],"a":1,"e":"4 in","r":"IRC R703.7.2.1"},{"id":"T10","d":"06-thermal-moisture","q":"Foundation walls that retain earth and enclose interior spaces below grade must, at minimum, be:","o":["Waterproofed in all cases","Dampproofed","Insulated to R-10","Covered with drainage board only"],"a":1,"e":"Dampproofed. Waterproofing is required where a high water table or severe soil-water conditions exist","r":"IRC R406.1, R406.2"},{"id":"T11","d":"06-thermal-moisture","q":"Under the IRC prescriptive energy table, the minimum ceiling insulation for Climate Zone 2 (Phoenix area) is:","o":["R-30","R-38","R-49","R-60"],"a":1,"e":"R-38","r":"IRC Table N1102.1.2"},{"id":"T12","d":"06-thermal-moisture","q":"In Climate Zone 2, the maximum fenestration SHGC under the IRC prescriptive path is:","o":["0.25","0.30","0.40","0.55"],"a":0,"e":"0.25 (with a U-factor of 0.40)","r":"IRC Table N1102.1.2"},{"id":"T13","d":"06-thermal-moisture","q":"Wall flashing should be installed:","o":["With the lower piece lapped over the upper piece","Shingle-fashion, with upper layers lapped over lower layers to direct water outward","Only at the top of windows","Behind the framing"],"a":1,"e":"","r":"IRC R703.4"},{"id":"T14","d":"06-thermal-moisture","q":"Under OSHA, a \"low-slope roof\" has a slope of:","o":["2 in 12 or less","3 in 12 or less","4 in 12 or less","6 in 12 or less"],"a":2,"e":"4 in 12 or less","r":"1926.500(b)"},{"id":"T15","d":"06-thermal-moisture","q":"Through-penetrations of fire-resistance-rated walls are protected by:","o":["Fireblocking of 2-in nominal lumber","Draftstopping","Approved through-penetration firestop systems","Vapor retarders"],"a":2,"e":"Firestop systems tested to ASTM E814/UL 1479","r":"IBC 714"},{"id":"D1","d":"07-doors-windows","q":"Under the IRC, an emergency escape and rescue opening must have a minimum net clear opening of ___ (except at grade floor openings).","o":["4.4 sq ft","5.0 sq ft","5.7 sq ft","6.0 sq ft"],"a":2,"e":"5.7 sq ft (5.0 sq ft for grade-floor or below-grade openings)","r":"IRC R310.2.1"},{"id":"D2","d":"07-doors-windows","q":"The sill of an emergency escape and rescue window may be no higher than ___ above the floor.","o":["36 in","42 in","44 in","48 in"],"a":2,"e":"44 in","r":"IRC R310.2.2"},{"id":"D3","d":"07-doors-windows","q":"Minimum net clear height and width for an emergency escape opening are:","o":["20 in high / 24 in wide","24 in high / 20 in wide","22 in high / 22 in wide","24 in high / 24 in wide"],"a":1,"e":"24 in high, 20 in wide","r":"IRC R310.2.1"},{"id":"D4","d":"07-doors-windows","q":"The required egress door for a dwelling must provide a clear width of at least ___ and a clear height of at least 78 in.","o":["30 in","32 in","34 in","36 in"],"a":1,"e":"32 in","r":"IRC R311.2"},{"id":"D5","d":"07-doors-windows","q":"Glazing adjacent to a door is a hazardous location when it is within a ___ arc of either vertical edge of the door (closed) and its bottom edge is less than 60 in above the floor.","o":["12 in","18 in","24 in","36 in"],"a":2,"e":"24 in","r":"IRC R308.4.2; IBC 2406.4.2"},{"id":"D6","d":"07-doors-windows","q":"A large fixed window requires safety glazing only when ALL of these apply: pane larger than 9 sq ft, bottom edge less than 18 in above the floor, top edge more than 36 in above the floor, and:","o":["It faces south","A walking surface is within 36 in horizontally","It is within 10 ft of a door","It is double-pane"],"a":1,"e":"","r":"IRC R308.4.3; IBC 2406.4.3"},{"id":"D7","d":"07-doors-windows","q":"Glazing in walls enclosing a bathtub or shower is a hazardous location when its bottom edge is less than ___ above the standing or walking surface.","o":["36 in","48 in","60 in","72 in"],"a":2,"e":"60 in","r":"IRC R308.4.5"},{"id":"D8","d":"07-doors-windows","q":"A door between a dwelling and an attached garage must be solid wood or steel at least 1⅜ in thick, or:","o":["A 20-minute fire-rated door","A 45-minute fire-rated door","A 1-hour fire-rated door","Any exterior door"],"a":0,"e":"20-minute fire-rated","r":"IRC R302.5.1"},{"id":"D9","d":"07-doors-windows","q":"Under the IBC, egress doors must swing in the direction of egress travel when serving a room or area with an occupant load of:","o":["10 or more","30 or more","50 or more","100 or more"],"a":2,"e":"50 or more (or any Group H)","r":"IBC 1010.1.2.1"},{"id":"D10","d":"07-doors-windows","q":"In a dwelling, where an operable window opening is more than 72 in above finished grade, the lowest part of the clear opening must be at least ___ above the floor unless a fall prevention or opening-limiting device is installed.","o":["18 in","24 in","30 in","36 in"],"a":1,"e":"24 in","r":"IRC R312.2.1"},{"id":"D11","d":"07-doors-windows","q":"The performance label on windows and doors showing U-factor and SHGC comes from:","o":["ANSI","NFRC","UL","AAMA"],"a":1,"e":"National Fenestration Rating Council","r":"IRC N1101.10.3"},{"id":"D12","d":"07-doors-windows","q":"Under the IBC, panic or fire exit hardware is required on swinging egress doors with locks or latches in Group A and E occupancies with an occupant load of:","o":["10 or more","30 or more","50 or more","300 or more"],"a":2,"e":"50 or more","r":"IBC 1010.1.10"},{"id":"F1","d":"08-finishes","q":"Fire-rated Type X gypsum board is typically:","o":["¼ in thick","⅜ in thick","½ in thick","⅝ in thick"],"a":3,"e":"⅝ in","r":"IRC R302.6 / ASTM C1396"},{"id":"F2","d":"08-finishes","q":"When screws attach gypsum board to wall framing at 16 in o.c., the maximum fastener spacing is:","o":["7 in o.c.","8 in o.c.","12 in o.c.","16 in o.c."],"a":3,"e":"16 in o.c. on walls (12 in on ceilings)","r":"IRC Table R702.3.5"},{"id":"F3","d":"08-finishes","q":"Which material is NOT permitted as a tile backer on shower walls under the 2018 IRC?","o":["Fiber-cement backer board","Cement board","Glass mat water-resistant gypsum backer","Water-resistant gypsum board (\"green board\")"],"a":3,"e":"","r":"IRC R702.3.7, R702.4.2"},{"id":"F4","d":"08-finishes","q":"A Class A interior finish has a flame spread index of:","o":["0–25","26–75","76–200","201–450"],"a":0,"e":"","r":"IBC 803.1.2"},{"id":"F5","d":"08-finishes","q":"Under the IRC, the garage side of a dwelling-garage separation wall must have at least:","o":["½ in gypsum board","⅝ in Type X gypsum board","1-hour rated assembly","⅜ in plywood"],"a":0,"e":"½ in gypsum board (⅝ in Type X on the ceiling when habitable rooms are above)","r":"IRC R302.6, Table R302.6"},{"id":"F6","d":"08-finishes","q":"The correct order of coats in three-coat plaster is:","o":["Brown, scratch, finish","Scratch, brown, finish","Finish, brown, scratch","Scratch, finish, brown"],"a":1,"e":"","r":"IRC R703.7; IBC 2512"},{"id":"F7","d":"08-finishes","q":"Exterior three-coat Portland cement plaster (stucco) over lath has a total thickness of about:","o":["⅜ in","½ in","⅞ in","1½ in"],"a":2,"e":"About ⅞ in","r":"IRC R703.7, Table R702.1(1)"},{"id":"F8","d":"08-finishes","q":"The gypsum board finish level that calls for a skim coat over the entire surface, for gloss paint or critical lighting, is:","o":["Level 2","Level 3","Level 4","Level 5"],"a":3,"e":"Level 5","r":"GA-214"},{"id":"F9","d":"08-finishes","q":"Under the IRC, wall and ceiling finishes must have a flame spread index of not more than:","o":["25","75","200","450"],"a":2,"e":"200 (smoke-developed index max 450)","r":"IRC R302.9"},{"id":"F10","d":"08-finishes","q":"Before installing solid hardwood flooring, the installer should:","o":["Seal the subfloor with oil-based paint","Acclimate the flooring and leave an expansion gap at the walls","Glue it tight to the walls","Install it before the HVAC is running"],"a":1,"e":"","r":"Carpentry and Building Construction"},{"id":"S1","d":"09-safety","q":"Under 29 CFR 1926 Subpart M, fall protection is required for employees on a walking/working surface with an unprotected side or edge at what height or more?","o":["4 ft","6 ft","10 ft","15 ft"],"a":1,"e":"6 ft","r":"1926.501(b)(1)"},{"id":"S2","d":"09-safety","q":"The top edge height of a guardrail system's top rail must be:","o":["36 in ± 3 in","39 in ± 2 in","42 in ± 3 in","45 in ± 3 in"],"a":2,"e":"42 in plus or minus 3 in","r":"1926.502(b)(1)"},{"id":"S3","d":"09-safety","q":"A guardrail top rail must withstand a force of at least ___ applied within 2 in of the top edge, outward or downward.","o":["100 lb","150 lb","200 lb","300 lb"],"a":2,"e":"200 lb","r":"1926.502(b)(3)"},{"id":"S4","d":"09-safety","q":"Anchorages used for attaching personal fall arrest equipment must support at least ___ per employee attached, unless designed by a qualified person with a 2:1 safety factor.","o":["1,800 lb","3,000 lb","5,000 lb","10,000 lb"],"a":2,"e":"5,000 lb","r":"1926.502(d)(15)"},{"id":"S5","d":"09-safety","q":"An excavation requires a protective system (sloping, shoring or shielding) when it is ___ or deeper, unless made entirely in stable rock.","o":["4 ft","5 ft","6 ft","8 ft"],"a":1,"e":"5 ft","r":"1926.652(a)(1)"},{"id":"S6","d":"09-safety","q":"In a trench 4 ft or more deep, a stairway, ladder, ramp or other safe means of egress must be located so lateral travel to it is no more than:","o":["15 ft","25 ft","50 ft","75 ft"],"a":1,"e":"25 ft","r":"1926.651(c)(2)"},{"id":"S7","d":"09-safety","q":"Excavated material (spoil) must be kept back from the edge of the excavation at least:","o":["1 ft","2 ft","3 ft","5 ft"],"a":1,"e":"2 ft, or retained by a device that prevents it falling in","r":"1926.651(j)(2)"},{"id":"S8","d":"09-safety","q":"The maximum allowable slope for an excavation less than 20 ft deep in Type C soil is:","o":["¾H:1V","1H:1V","1½H:1V","2H:1V"],"a":2,"e":"1½ horizontal to 1 vertical (34°)","r":"1926 Subpart P, Appendix B, Table B-1"},{"id":"S9","d":"09-safety","q":"Type A soil has an unconfined compressive strength of:","o":["0.5 tsf or less","0.5 to 1.0 tsf","1.0 to 1.5 tsf","1.5 tsf or greater"],"a":3,"e":"1.5 tons per square foot or greater","r":"1926 Subpart P, Appendix A"},{"id":"S10","d":"09-safety","q":"Protective systems for excavations deeper than ___ must be designed by a registered professional engineer.","o":["10 ft","15 ft","20 ft","25 ft"],"a":2,"e":"20 ft","r":"1926.652(b) and (c)"},{"id":"S11","d":"09-safety","q":"Who must inspect excavations, adjacent areas and protective systems daily and before the start of each shift?","o":["The project owner","A registered professional engineer","A competent person","The building official"],"a":2,"e":"A competent person","r":"1926.651(k)(1)"},{"id":"S12","d":"09-safety","q":"When a portable ladder is used to access an upper landing, its side rails must extend at least ___ above the landing.","o":["2 ft","3 ft","4 ft","5 ft"],"a":1,"e":"3 ft","r":"1926.1053(b)(1)"},{"id":"S13","d":"09-safety","q":"A non-self-supporting ladder with a 16 ft working length should have its base set out from the wall:","o":["2 ft","3 ft","4 ft","5 ft"],"a":2,"e":"4 ft (1 ft horizontal per 4 ft of working length)","r":"1926.1053(b)(5)(i)"},{"id":"S14","d":"09-safety","q":"A stairway or ladder must be provided at every point of access where there is a break in elevation of ___ or more and no ramp, runway or personnel hoist.","o":["12 in","19 in","24 in","30 in"],"a":1,"e":"19 in","r":"1926.1051(a)"},{"id":"S15","d":"09-safety","q":"Each scaffold and scaffold component must support, without failure, its own weight and at least ___ the maximum intended load.","o":["2 times","3 times","4 times","5 times"],"a":2,"e":"4 times","r":"1926.451(a)(1)"},{"id":"S16","d":"09-safety","q":"Employees on a scaffold must be protected from falls when the platform is more than ___ above a lower level.","o":["6 ft","8 ft","10 ft","15 ft"],"a":2,"e":"10 ft","r":"1926.451(g)(1)"},{"id":"S17","d":"09-safety","q":"When a masonry wall is being built, a limited access zone must be established. Its width is:","o":["Equal to the wall height","Wall height plus 2 ft","Wall height plus 4 ft","Twice the wall height"],"a":2,"e":"Height of the wall plus 4 ft, running the entire length of the wall","r":"1926.706(a)"},{"id":"S18","d":"09-safety","q":"During steel erection, workers other than connectors and those in a controlled decking zone must be protected from falls at heights over:","o":["6 ft","10 ft","15 ft","30 ft"],"a":2,"e":"15 ft","r":"1926.760(a)(1)"},{"id":"S19","d":"09-safety","q":"All columns must be anchored by a minimum of ___ anchor rods (anchor bolts).","o":["2","3","4","6"],"a":2,"e":"4","r":"1926.755(a)(1)"},{"id":"S20","d":"09-safety","q":"On a construction site, 120-volt, single-phase, 15- and 20-amp receptacles that are not part of the permanent wiring must be protected by GFCIs or by:","o":["A lockout/tagout program","An assured equipment grounding conductor program","Weatherproof covers","A licensed electrician's daily inspection"],"a":1,"e":"An assured equipment grounding conductor program","r":"1926.404(b)(1)"},{"id":"S21","d":"09-safety","q":"A fire extinguisher rated not less than 2A must be provided for each ___ of protected building area, with travel distance not exceeding 100 ft.","o":["1,500 sq ft","2,500 sq ft","3,000 sq ft","5,000 sq ft"],"a":2,"e":"3,000 sq ft","r":"1926.150(c)(1)(i)"},{"id":"S22","d":"09-safety","q":"A personal fall arrest system must be rigged so an employee can neither free fall more than ___ nor contact any lower level.","o":["3½ ft","4 ft","6 ft","10 ft"],"a":2,"e":"6 ft","r":"1926.502(d)(16)(iii)"},{"id":"G1","d":"10-general-building-code","q":"Under the IBC, a permit becomes invalid if the authorized work is not commenced within:","o":["90 days","120 days","180 days","1 year"],"a":2,"e":"180 days after issuance","r":"IBC 105.5"},{"id":"G2","d":"10-general-building-code","q":"An office building is classified as occupancy Group:","o":["A-3","B","M","R-2"],"a":1,"e":"Business","r":"IBC 304.1"},{"id":"G3","d":"10-general-building-code","q":"An apartment building with more than two dwelling units is occupancy Group:","o":["R-1","R-2","R-3","R-4"],"a":1,"e":"R-2","r":"IBC 310.4"},{"id":"G4","d":"10-general-building-code","q":"A hotel, where occupants are primarily transient, is occupancy Group:","o":["R-1","R-2","B","A-2"],"a":0,"e":"R-1","r":"IBC 310.3"},{"id":"G5","d":"10-general-building-code","q":"Which type of construction allows structural elements and walls of any material permitted by the code?","o":["Type I","Type II","Type III","Type V"],"a":3,"e":"Type V","r":"IBC 602.5"},{"id":"G6","d":"10-general-building-code","q":"In a commercial building (IBC), the required guard height at an open side of a walking surface more than 30 in above the floor below is:","o":["34 in","36 in","38 in","42 in"],"a":3,"e":"42 in (the IRC requires 36 in)","r":"IBC 1015.3"},{"id":"G7","d":"10-general-building-code","q":"Required guards must not have openings that allow passage of a sphere:","o":["2 in in diameter","4 in in diameter","4⅜ in in diameter","6 in in diameter"],"a":1,"e":"4 in (the triangle at a stair tread and riser is 6 in)","r":"IBC 1015.4; IRC R312.1.3"},{"id":"G8","d":"10-general-building-code","q":"Handrail height, measured above stair tread nosings, must be:","o":["30 to 34 in","32 to 36 in","34 to 38 in","36 to 42 in"],"a":2,"e":"34 to 38 in","r":"IBC 1014.2; IRC R311.7.8.1"},{"id":"G9","d":"10-general-building-code","q":"For a commercial (IBC) stair, the maximum riser height and minimum tread depth are:","o":["7¾ in / 10 in","7 in / 11 in","8 in / 9 in","7½ in / 10½ in"],"a":1,"e":"7 in max riser, 11 in min tread","r":"IBC 1011.5.2"},{"id":"G10","d":"10-general-building-code","q":"For a dwelling stair under the IRC, the maximum riser height and minimum tread depth are:","o":["7 in / 11 in","7¾ in / 10 in","8¼ in / 9 in","7½ in / 10½ in"],"a":1,"e":"7¾ in / 10 in","r":"IRC R311.7.5"},{"id":"G11","d":"10-general-building-code","q":"Special inspectors required by IBC Chapter 17 must be employed by:","o":["The general contractor","The building official","The owner or the owner's authorized agent","The material supplier"],"a":2,"e":"not the contractor responsible for the work","r":"IBC 1704.2"},{"id":"G12","d":"10-general-building-code","q":"The bottom of footings must be at least ___ below undisturbed ground surface (and below the frost line).","o":["6 in","12 in","18 in","24 in"],"a":1,"e":"12 in","r":"IBC 1809.4; IRC R403.1.4"},{"id":"G13","d":"10-general-building-code","q":"Without a soils report, the presumptive load-bearing value for clay, sandy clay or silty clay soils is:","o":["1,000 psf","1,500 psf","2,000 psf","3,000 psf"],"a":1,"e":"1,500 psf","r":"IBC Table 1806.2; IRC Table R401.4.1"},{"id":"G14","d":"10-general-building-code","q":"Under the IRC, the uniform live load for sleeping rooms is:","o":["20 psf","30 psf","40 psf","50 psf"],"a":1,"e":"30 psf (other living areas are 40 psf)","r":"IRC Table R301.5"},{"id":"G15","d":"10-general-building-code","q":"Under the IRC, habitable rooms (except kitchens) must have a floor area of at least ___ and no horizontal dimension less than 7 ft.","o":["50 sq ft","70 sq ft","80 sq ft","100 sq ft"],"a":1,"e":"70 sq ft","r":"IRC R304"},{"id":"G16","d":"10-general-building-code","q":"During construction, at least one standpipe must be provided (in buildings required to have standpipes) before construction exceeds ___ above the lowest level of fire department vehicle access.","o":["30 ft","40 ft","55 ft","75 ft"],"a":1,"e":"40 ft","r":"IBC 3311.1"},{"id":"G17","d":"10-general-building-code","q":"Under the IBC, a corridor serving an occupant load of 50 or more must be at least:","o":["36 in wide","44 in wide","48 in wide","60 in wide"],"a":1,"e":"44 in","r":"IBC Table 1020.2"}],"docs":{"plan":"<h2>3-Day Plan for Test Friday 2026-11-20</h2>\n<h3>First, the must-have</h3>\n<p>You need <strong>bound</strong> copies of the 2018 IBC, the 2018 IRC and 29 CFR 1926 in the room, with permanent tabs only. If you don't have them yet, get them today. Bound OSHA 1926 is the easiest to find locally. The ICC books may need same-day pickup or a prep-company tabbed bundle. Without the books, the Safety and Code points (21 items) get hard.</p>\n<h3>Wednesday 11/18: Lookup speed (Safety + Code, 21 items)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Tab OSHA 1926 by subpart: C, E, K, L (scaffolds), M (fall protection), P (excavations), Q (concrete/masonry), R (steel erection), X (ladders)</li>\n<li>Tab the IBC: Ch. 3 (occupancy), 5 (heights/areas), 6 (construction types), 10 (egress), 14–15, 16 (structural), 17 (special inspections), 18–19, 21–23, 25</li>\n<li>Tab the IRC: R301 (design criteria), R302, R308, R310, R311, R403, R502, R602, R802, R905</li>\n<li>Drill: all questions in <a href=\"#/study/09-safety\">Safety</a> and <a href=\"#/study/10-general-building-code\">General Building Code</a>, then 40 OpenExamPrep questions. Time every lookup; the target is under 90 seconds.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Thursday 11/19: Knowledge domains (Concrete, Metals, Carpentry, Masonry, Sitework, 58 items)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Concrete: water-cement ratio, slump, curing, air entrainment, cold and hot weather placement, rebar cover, cylinder testing</li>\n<li>Metals: steel joist handling and erection, bridging, Subpart R connector and decking rules</li>\n<li>Masonry: mortar types (M S N O K), CMU sizes, grout, reinforcement, cold and hot weather work</li>\n<li>Sitework: soil types (A, B, C), sloping and benching, trench boxes, pipe bedding</li>\n<li>Carpentry: framing spans, headers, sheathing, fasteners</li>\n<li>Study: <a href=\"#/study/02-concrete\">Concrete</a>, <a href=\"#/study/04-metals\">Metals</a>, <a href=\"#/study/05-carpentry\">Carpentry</a>, <a href=\"#/study/03-masonry\">Masonry</a>, <a href=\"#/study/01-sitework\">Sitework</a>, then skim <a href=\"#/study/06-thermal-moisture\">Thermal</a>, <a href=\"#/study/07-doors-windows\">Doors &amp; Windows</a>, <a href=\"#/study/08-finishes\">Finishes</a></li>\n<li>Drill: <a href=\"#/exam\">Practice Exam 01</a>, timed (240 min). Log every miss in <code>practice/missed-log.md</code>. Retake misses with <a href=\"#/exam\">Practice Exam 02</a> if time allows.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Friday 11/20: Test day</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Morning: review only the missed-question log</li>\n<li>Pace: about 2.4 minutes per question. Answer the knowledge questions first and skip the long lookups, then come back for them.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Long-Term Plan (8 weeks, for retake or deeper guide build-out)</h2>\n<p>Weighting follows the content outline, so more weeks go to the heavier domains and to the study-only books, where open-book lookup won't save you.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Week</th>\n<th>Focus</th>\n<th>Items on exam</th>\n<th>Output</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Setup: get books, tab IRC/IBC/OSHA, baseline 50-question practice test</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Baseline score, missed-question log started</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Safety (OSHA 1926)</td>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Tab map + 40-question set</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Concrete</td>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>Notes from PCA + IBC Ch. 19, 40-question set</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Metals</td>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>SJI notes + Subpart R, 40-question set</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Carpentry + Doors &amp; Windows</td>\n<td>17</td>\n<td>Notes + 40-question set</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Masonry + Thermal &amp; Moisture</td>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>Notes + 40-question set</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Sitework + Finishes + General Building Code</td>\n<td>24</td>\n<td>Notes + 40-question set</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Full timed 100-question exams (240 min), review misses</td>\n<td>100</td>\n<td>Two full simulations at 80% or better</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>SRE (Gmetrix)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Can be done anytime, online</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>Rules</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Every missed question gets logged in <code>practice/missed-log.md</code> with its domain and the reference location.</li>\n<li>Lookup drill: you get 2.4 minutes per question, so train to under 90 seconds per code lookup.</li>\n</ul>","sources":"<p>Arizona doesn't publish an IDE-style standards document for contractors. The equivalent is the <strong>PSI Candidate Information Bulletin (CIB)</strong> plus the <strong>trade-specific content outline</strong> it links to. Together they define what's tested, the item counts, and which references are allowed.</p>\n<h2>Primary (authoritative)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>What it gives you</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>PSI Arizona Contractor CIB</td>\n<td>Exam process, fees, retake rules, reference rules, links to content outlines</td>\n<td>https://test-takers.psiexams.com/api/content/bulletin/5617</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PSI Arizona contractor portal</td>\n<td>Scheduling + content outline downloads (B-1/B-2/KB-1/KB-2 Residential Commercial Contractor Content Outline)</td>\n<td>https://test-takers.psiexams.com/azcon</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AZ ROC — Applying for a License</td>\n<td>Which exams each classification needs, SRE details, NASCLA option</td>\n<td>https://roc.az.gov/licensing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AZ ROC — License Classifications</td>\n<td>KB-2 scope of work</td>\n<td>https://roc.az.gov/license-classifications</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AZ ROC — Applicant Education Seminar</td>\n<td>Free monthly online seminar on the application process</td>\n<td>https://azroc.gov/applying-for-a-license</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>Contacts</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>PSI scheduling: (855) 744-0310</li>\n<li>AZ ROC Licensing: 1-877-692-9762</li>\n<li>Gmetrix (SRE): 801-323-5800</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Key rules from the CIB and AZ ROC</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>B-1, B-2, KB-1 and KB-2 were combined into <strong>one trade exam</strong> (effective 6/20/2024).</li>\n<li>The SRE is required for new applicants who haven't been a qualifying party on an Arizona license in the last five years. It is online only through Gmetrix and cannot be waived.</li>\n<li>Retakes: wait 30 days after a failed attempt. After 3 failures, wait 90 days before each further retake.</li>\n<li>Scores are valid for 2 years. The application and score report must reach AZ ROC within that window.</li>\n<li>Reference books may be highlighted, underlined and tabbed with <strong>permanent</strong> tabs only. No writing during the exam.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Exam waiver: check this first</h2>\n<p>A.R.S. § 32-1122(F): the registrar <strong>shall waive</strong> the examination requirement if ROC records show the qualifying party \"is currently or has previously been a qualifying party for a licensee in this state in the same classification within the preceding five years.\" Subsection E.1 also waives work-experience documentation on the same basis.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Statute: https://www.azleg.gov/ars/32/01122.htm</li>\n<li>Rule: A.A.C. R4-9-106(E)</li>\n<li>Form: AZ ROC In-State Waiver Request form (roc.az.gov)</li>\n<li>Confirm with ROC Licensing at (602) 542-1525 before paying for or sitting an exam.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>To do</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>[ ] Download the official B-1/B-2/KB-1/KB-2 content outline PDF from the PSI portal and save it to <code>docs/official/</code></li>\n<li>[ ] Verify the item counts in <code>01-content-outline.md</code> against it</li>\n<li>[ ] Note the outline's revision date here</li>\n</ul>","refs":"<h2>Allowed in the exam room (3)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Book</th>\n<th>Free to read online</th>\n<th>Buy</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>29 CFR Part 1926 — OSHA Construction Standards</td>\n<td>https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-29/subtitle-B/chapter-XVII/part-1926</td>\n<td>Bound copy required in the room</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2018 International Residential Code (IRC)</td>\n<td>https://codes.iccsafe.org (free read-only view)</td>\n<td>ICC Shop / PSI Online Store</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2018 International Building Code (IBC)</td>\n<td>https://codes.iccsafe.org (free read-only view)</td>\n<td>ICC Shop / PSI Online Store</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>Study-only (questions are written from these, but you can't bring them in)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Book</th>\n<th>Accepted editions</th>\n<th>Domains</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Design and Control of Concrete Mixtures (PCA)</td>\n<td>2011 or 2016</td>\n<td>Concrete</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pipe and Excavation Contracting</td>\n<td>1987 or 2011</td>\n<td>Sitework</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Handling and Erection of Steel Joists and Joist Girders (SJI)</td>\n<td>2006 or 2008</td>\n<td>Metals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Carpentry and Building Construction</td>\n<td>2010 or 2016</td>\n<td>Carpentry, Doors &amp; Windows, Finishes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Modern Masonry</td>\n<td>8th or 9th edition</td>\n<td>Masonry</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>Exam-room book rules</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Permanent tabs only (adhesive or stamped tabs that stay in the book)</li>\n<li>Highlighting and underlining are allowed</li>\n<li>No writing in the books</li>\n<li>Downloaded or printed references must be bound</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>SRE references</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A.R.S. Title 32, Chapter 10 (contractor statutes)</li>\n<li>A.A.C. Title 4, Chapter 9 (ROC rules)</li>\n<li>Provided inside the Gmetrix SRE course</li>\n</ul>","practice":"<h2>Free</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>What</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>OpenExamPrep — B-1/B-2/KB-1/KB-2</td>\n<td>100+ practice questions, no signup</td>\n<td>https://open-exam-prep.com/practice/us-azroc-b1-b2-residential-commercial</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>OpenExamPrep — AZ GC study guide</td>\n<td>14-chapter free guide organized by blueprint</td>\n<td>https://open-exam-prep.com/study-guides/az-general-contractor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AZ ROC Applicant Education Seminar</td>\n<td>Monthly, application process</td>\n<td>https://azroc.gov/applying-for-a-license</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>Paid (with exam simulators)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Provider</th>\n<th>Offering</th>\n<th>Link</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Contractor Training Center</td>\n<td>Online course, practice simulator, tabbed/highlighted book bundles</td>\n<td>https://contractortrainingcenter.com/collections/arizona-b</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RocketCert</td>\n<td>B-1/KB-1 online prep (about $299), book + tab bundles</td>\n<td>https://rocketcert.com/construction/arizona/b-1-kb-1-general-commercial-contractor-license</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1 Exam Prep</td>\n<td>KB-2 online prep course (about $295)</td>\n<td>https://1examprep.com/products/arizona-dual-residential-and-small-commercial-contractor-kb-2-exam-online-exam-prep</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>American Contractors Exam Services</td>\n<td>Live seminars, webinars, book tabbing</td>\n<td>https://www.examprep.org/?p=1497</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>iContractor</td>\n<td>B-1/B-2/KB-1/KB-2 test page with CIB links</td>\n<td>https://www.icontractor.net/test/b-1b-2kb-1kb-2-commercial-dual-general</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Arizona School of Real Estate &amp; Business</td>\n<td>Trade prep (16 hr)</td>\n<td>https://www.asreb.com/general-contractors/</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Caution: some free sites still list outdated info (for example the old \"Business Management\" exam, since replaced by the SRE, or claims that NASCLA isn't accepted). Trust PSI and AZ ROC first.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>Local in-person schools (Phoenix)</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>School</th>\n<th>Contact</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Arizona Contractor License Center (AZCLC)</td>\n<td>1010 E Jefferson St, Phoenix 85034 · 602-712-1515 · azclc.com</td>\n<td>Serving AZ contractors since 1972. Test prep, book store, application and bond help. 4.9 stars (394 reviews). Class schedule not visible online; call.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Contractors Exam Center (Contractors Exam, Inc.)</td>\n<td>Phoenix office 602-252-8311 · contractorsexam.com</td>\n<td>In-person and online courses, practice tests. Schedule not posted.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Arizona School of Real Estate &amp; Business (ASREB)</td>\n<td>asreb.com</td>\n<td>16-hour trade prep course</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Contractor School Online (\"Arizona Contractor School Online®\")</td>\n<td>(888) 993-9267 · contractorschoolonline.com</td>\n<td>Online only. Web-based trade practice tests, $97</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Questions to ask when calling: Is there a KB-2 (combined B-1/B-2/KB-1/KB-2) class before Friday 11/20? Do you sell or rent bound, pre-tabbed IBC, IRC and OSHA 1926 for same-day pickup? Is there practice-test access I can start tonight?</p>\n<h2>Library — findings (researched 2026-11-17)</h2>\n<p><strong>Bottom line: no Arizona library database found with an AZ ROC or KB-2 practice test.</strong> Library test-prep databases (LearningExpress, Peterson's) cover GED, SAT, ASVAB, CDL, nursing and similar exams, but not state contractor trade exams. Where libraries do help is with physical copies of the study-only books.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Library</th>\n<th>What it has</th>\n<th>Use for KB-2</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Phoenix Public Library</td>\n<td>Peterson's Test and Career Prep (free with a card)</td>\n<td>General test-taking only, no ROC content</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mesa CC Library</td>\n<td>Databases and eResources (MEID login)</td>\n<td>Possible reserve copies of construction texts; ask at the desk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ASU Libraries</td>\n<td>Engineering and construction collection</td>\n<td>Most likely place to find PCA <em>Design and Control of Concrete Mixtures</em>, <em>Modern Masonry</em>, <em>Carpentry and Building Construction</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>WorldCat (worldcat.org)</td>\n<td>Finds which libraries hold a book</td>\n<td>Search each study-only title by ISBN to locate copies in the Valley</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Edition warning: PCA's <em>Design and Control of Concrete Mixtures</em> is now on its <strong>17th edition</strong>. The exam accepts the <strong>2011 (15th) or 2016 (16th)</strong> edition. Library and bookstore copies may be the wrong one.</p>\n<h2>College and university — findings (researched 2026-11-17)</h2>\n<p><strong>Bottom line: no Arizona college or university offers an AZ ROC license-prep course.</strong> The closest school-based option is the <strong>Arizona School of Real Estate &amp; Business</strong> (a private school), which runs a 16-hour trade exam prep course. Colleges do offer credit courses that match the exam domains. They run a full semester, so they're useful for building the deeper guide later, not for this week.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>School</th>\n<th>Courses matching the exam blueprint</th>\n<th>Domains</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Mesa Community College (Construction Management/Trades, Dr. David Cain, 480-461-7377)</td>\n<td>CON106 Foundations of Concrete; CON140 Masonry; CON110–112 floor, wall and roof framing; CON213 Steel Framing; CON214 Roof Systems and Building Envelopes; CON215 Interior Finishes; CON216 Concrete Forms; CON103/CON271 Construction Safety; Construction Safety OSHA 30 certificate</td>\n<td>Nearly all 10 domains</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GateWay CC / South Mountain CC</td>\n<td>Construction Management and Construction Technology certificates and AAS</td>\n<td>General</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ASU Del E. Webb School of Construction</td>\n<td>CON 252 Building Construction Methods, Materials and Equipment; CON 383 Estimating; open to non-degree students</td>\n<td>Concrete, Metals, Masonry, Carpentry</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Collegiate-level text worth knowing: <em>Construction Materials, Methods, and Techniques</em> (Spence and Kultermann) is the standard materials-and-methods textbook in college syllabi. It covers the same ground as the study-only books.</p>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>For a near-term test date, the best free tools are <strong>OpenExamPrep's question bank</strong> plus the <strong>free online IBC, IRC and OSHA 1926</strong> for lookup drills. Libraries and colleges help the longer-term guide build-out, not this week.</p>","outline":"<p><strong>Source:</strong> Third-party summary of the PSI outline (OpenExamPrep). <strong>Not yet verified</strong> against the official PSI PDF. See <code>00-official-sources.md</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>100 questions</li>\n<li>240 minutes (2.4 minutes per question)</li>\n<li>70% to pass (70 correct)</li>\n<li>Open book: 29 CFR 1926, 2018 IRC, 2018 IBC</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Domain</th>\n<th>Items</th>\n<th>% of exam</th>\n<th>Primary reference</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Concrete</td>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>14%</td>\n<td>Design and Control of Concrete Mixtures (study only), IBC Ch. 19</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Metals</td>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>13%</td>\n<td>Handling and Erection of Steel Joists (study only), OSHA 1926 Subpart R</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Carpentry</td>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>Carpentry and Building Construction (study only), IRC/IBC Ch. 23</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Safety</td>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>12%</td>\n<td>29 CFR 1926 (in room)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Masonry</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>Modern Masonry (study only), IBC Ch. 21</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Thermal &amp; Moisture Protection</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>10%</td>\n<td>IBC Ch. 14–15, IRC Ch. 7–9</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Sitework</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>9%</td>\n<td>Pipe and Excavation Contracting (study only), OSHA 1926 Subpart P</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>General Building Code</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>9%</td>\n<td>2018 IBC (in room)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Finishes</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>6%</td>\n<td>IBC Ch. 25, IRC Ch. 7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Doors &amp; Windows</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>5%</td>\n<td>IBC Ch. 10/24, IRC R308/R310/R612</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td><strong>Total</strong></td>\n<td><strong>100</strong></td>\n<td><strong>100%</strong></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>How to use this</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The top four domains (Concrete, Metals, Carpentry, Safety) are <strong>51%</strong> of the exam.</li>\n<li>Safety and General Building Code (21 items) come straight from books you can bring in, so tab them well and those points come from lookup speed.</li>\n<li>Concrete, Metals, Masonry and Sitework draw on <strong>study-only</strong> books. Those points depend on what you actually know.</li>\n<li>The \"primary reference\" column is a working guess at chapter mapping and should be checked as each domain folder is built.</li>\n</ul>"}};
